{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'def run|if __name__|GOWIN|placement|gowin' tools/eval/gowin.py tools/eval/fpga.py\nls -ld formal/riscv-formal\ncat test/cosim/build.sh\nrg --files cores/bench/test\ncat .gitignore | tail -65\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py\ncores/bench/test/conftest.py\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\ncores/bench/test/_helpers.py\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py\ncores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n*.swp\n.idea/\n.vscode/\n\n# Claude Code workspace\n.claude/\ndocs/assets/\n\n# Local git worktrees (subagent-driven dev; not the orchestrator's experiments/worktrees)\n.worktrees/\n\n# Node deps for bench-fence pi extension (installed locally for typecheck;\n# pi handles installs at run time so the deps don't need to be committed).\nnode_modules/\n\n# Pi-coding-agent session storage inside per-rep clones.\n.pi-sessions/\n\n# Per-rep agent telemetry inside bench/ is now tracked: log.jsonl carries\n# the full per-iteration data (LUT4, Fmax, IPC, error class) that the\n# rolled-up summary.json doesn't surface, and agent.log is the model's\n# verbatim transcript — both are essential for reproducibility and\n# postmortems. Combined they're ~3-4 MB per rep × 9 reps = ~35 MB; cheap.\n# (Previously gitignored as \"regenerable\", but they're only regenerable\n# by re-running the bench at considerable wall time and cost.)\n\n# repo.bundle (each rep's full git history, task-7 disk hygiene) is NOT\n# tracked, unlike orchestrator.log: a bundle runs about 45 MB binary per\n# rep, and at full compute-matrix scale that would bloat the git history\n# by gigabytes. It stays on disk as a local forensics artifact instead,\n# and tools/bench/gc.py archives (or recreates) it before deleting the\n# clone it was bundled from, so the content is not lost.\nbench/**/repo.bundle\n\n# agent.full.log.gz: the verbatim agent transcript. The tracked agent.log\n# next to it has tool-output fields capped (tools/bench/transcript.py),\n# which cut the published transcripts ~60%; the full stream stays on\n# disk for forensics, same policy as repo.bundle.\nbench/**/agent.full.log.gz\n\n# env.json (per-rep environment snapshot: PATH + resolved EDA tool paths)\n# IS tracked, decided at the first v2 rep publish (e1a static, 2026-07-16):\n# it is tiny, contains no secrets, and pins which toolchain binaries\n# produced each rep's numbers.\n\n# Throwaway harness-verification runs (one-model YAMLs + their output\n# trees). These are reliability checks, not benchmark data — output is\n# regenerable and the YAMLs only encode \"rerun this single model to\n# confirm a fix landed\". Naming convention: `*-verify` for output dirs,\n# `models-*-verify.yaml` for the throwaway model YAML.\nbench/*-verify/\nbench/smoke-*/\ntools/bench/models-*-verify.yaml\n\n# Aborted/partial bench output preserved locally with a debug-tag suffix\n# (e.g. `gpt-5_5.failed-pwd-bug/`, `gpt-5_5.validate-v3/`, sibling\n# `results.jsonl.<tag>`). They document a known-broken state for the\n# author's own postmortem; the canonical results live in the un-suffixed\n# per-model dirs.\nbench/*.failed-*\nbench/*.validate-*\nbench/*.prev-*\nbench/*.stale-*\nbench/*.partial-*\nbench/results.jsonl.*\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
