{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Budget iterative DIV/REM against the full retirement gap: eight iteration clocks plus launch/consume exhaust the 10-cycle RVFI liveness allowance, so a preceding load-use bubble stretches the gap to 11 cycles and fails liveness_ch0.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (improvement, +10.40%): With combinational IF/ID, a 128-entry bimodal BHT and early JAL targets improve CoreMark by eliminating the single issue bubble on correctly predicted transfers, using EX comparison of actual_next_pc against the latched predicted_next_pc to flush only on misprediction.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (improvement, +458.74%): Reusing eight restoring DIV/REM steps over four clocks shortens the combinational ALU path enough to outweigh added divide stalls, while launch/consume and a preceding load-use bubble still fit the 10-cycle retirement limit.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r3s1 (improvement, +9.24%): Reducing the shared DIV/REM chain from eight to six restoring steps per clock (five six-bit iterations plus a final two-bit iteration) improves Fmax without changing CoreMark's 4,202,272 cycles, but shifts the critical path to register-file/redirect/hazard logic ending at ID/EX, limiting further gains from divider retiming alone.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r4s0 (regression, +17.60%): Separating IF/ID and ID/EX payloads from squash control shortens the recovery-to-decode/load-use path without extra CoreMark cycles, but requires clearing side-effect controls as well as valid and qualifying raw-source load-use checks with imem_ready.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r4s2 (improvement, +17.89%): Capturing the guarded BRANCH/JAL target-selection bit lets EX recover via direction XOR (except offset +4) instead of a selected 32-bit next-PC comparison, shortening the redirect path without changing CoreMark cycles while JALR keeps a separate full-width comparison.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r5s0 (regression, +17.50%): Registering 34-bit 3D at launch lets three radix-4 steps with parallel subtractors shorten the six-stage restoring recurrence without extending divide latency, while the prototype's nearly tied register-file-to-memory path limits further gains from divider-only timing changes.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r5s1 (improvement, +19.44%): Predecoding the first two restoring DIV/REM steps at launch with a small-divisor case network shortens feedback from six subtract/select stages to five and removes the final-step mux, improving median Fmax from 40.459 to 48.327 MHz while preserving six iteration clocks and CoreMark's 4,202,272 cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r5s2 (regression, +4.67%): Decoupling decode availability from io_imemReady lets a four-entry fixed-head queue hide load-use, dmem and divide stalls, but the prototype's 5.3% CoreMark cycle reduction leaves the unchanged divider critical path limiting Fmax.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r6s1 (improvement, +11.57%): Direct divide retirement allows eight four-step iterations within the 10-cycle retirement bound, while a dedicated EX/MEM effective_addr removes the exposed multiplier-to-dmem path; CoreMark's measured interval contains no divides, so increased divide spacing adds no benchmark cycles and the Fmax gain translates directly into fitness.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r7s0 (improvement, +17.72%): On Gowin, coupling a scalar-flop register file with balanced OR-tree reads and next-owner forwarding selectors registered at ID/EX removes complementary BSRAM-launch and EX tag-comparison bottlenecks that limit either change alone.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
