{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r6s0.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261002-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Move M-extension selection ahead of arithmetic\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 115.00 at median Gowin Fmax 48.327 MHz and\n  4,202,272 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst path crosses five\n  restoring subtract/select steps, while prior r5s0 timing exposed a\n  nearly tied register-file/multiplier/result-selection path. Local\n  prototypes confirm that shortening only division reaches 49.619 MHz\n  and moves the bottleneck into multiplication, below the required\n  50.54913 MHz threshold. Sharing only multiplication leaves division\n  limiting. The promising angle is one coordinated ALU mapping change:\n  move wide M-extension result selection to the arithmetic inputs,\n  addressing both limiting paths without changing instruction latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Eliminate late wide result selection in the existing M-extension ALU.\n  Use non-restoring division to replace each subtract-and-restore mux\n  with one selected-divisor addition, and use operand sign extension\n  to select multiplication semantics before one shared product. Both\n  are local arithmetic substitutions in alu.sv: keep the pipeline,\n  launch plus six iteration clocks, consume protocol and nret=1 intact.\n\n  Retain the current exact two-bit launch prefix, signed input magnitude\n  conversion, quotient initialization and exception metadata. Widen the\n  running remainder to 33 bits, interpreted as two's-complement signed.\n  At the same launch edge capture D as today and negative_divisor_q =\n  33'b0 - {1'b0,D}; compute it from the current operand magnitude, never\n  the old divisor register. Initialize the remainder by zero-extending\n  the existing two-bit remainder_prefix into 33 bits.\n\n  Each busy clock still consumes five dividend bits, through five serial\n  combinational non-restoring steps. For each step, choose the 33-bit\n  addend from the incoming remainder sign: +D when R[32]=1, otherwise\n  the registered -D. Compute Rnext = {R[31:0],Q[31]} + addend in exactly\n  33 bits and Qnext = {Q[30:0],\"'!Rnext[32]}. Capture step 5 on each of\n  the six busy edges and assert done at iteration index 5 as before.\n  There is no wide restore mux after each addition. For nonzero D the\n  invariant is -D <= signed(R) < D; its corrected unsigned remainder\n  is R+D if R is negative, otherwise R. The 33-bit modular arithmetic\n  represents each next signed remainder exactly, even when shifting R\n  momentarily needs more bits before the addend cancels them.\n\n  Apply that final correction combinationally to the REM/REMU magnitude\n  before the existing dividend-sign correction. Quotient bits are already\n  in ordinary binary, so DIV/DIVU need no quotient conversion. Preserve\n  explicit division-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 overrides, all result retention,\n  reset cancellation, repeated-request rejection and fixed completion\n  timing. Do not add a preparation or final-correction cycle.\n\n  Replace mul_ss, mul_uu and mul_su with one signed 33-by-33 multiplication.\n  Set Aext={signed_a && a[31],a}, with signed_a true only for MULH/MULHSU;\n  set Bext={signed_b && b[31],b}, with signed_b true only for MULH. Declare\n  both extended operands signed [32:0] and their product signed [65:0].\n  MUL reads product[31:0]; MULH, MULHU and MULHSU all read product[63:32].\n  The two extra product bits are intentionally unused. This covers the\n  complete unsigned operand range and signed high products without a\n  post-product correction subtractor or selection among three products.\n  MUL'\"'s low half is identical for every signedness interpretation.\n\n  Preserve every RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formula and the same handshake in\n  that build; exclude real divider arithmetic there. Keep all non-M ALU\n  operations, EX operand snapshots, bus holds, branch prediction, memory\n  behavior, decoder checks, RVFI ports/order and retirement-gap limits.\n  This hypothesis changes arithmetic mapping only, with no extra stages\n  or instructions in flight and no contract or evaluation changes.\n\n  The benefit should come from eliminating repeated wide restore muxes\n  and reducing multiplier resources and output selection. Independent\n  prototypes measured two DSP blocks for the shared product versus six\n  originally, and exposed why both limiting cones need the same treatment.\n  Risks include a new remainder-correction/sign path, launch negation,\n  sign-control fanout and placement variation. Final combined timing and\n  fitness must be measured; isolated timing results are not additive.\n\n  A local combinational SAT miter proved the non-restoring step's quotient\n  decision and corrected remainder equivalent to the original restoring\n  step for arbitrary nonzero D, R<D, incoming quotient bit and either\n  signed representation of R. This does not prove the full multiplier\n  or replace real-arithmetic testing. Validate both real and ALTOPS ALU\n  and pipeline suites, unchanged formal, stalled full-RVFI cosimulation,\n  and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks. Retain every\n  six-iteration and startup/retirement-gap assertion. The identical cycle\n  schedule should preserve 4,202,272 bracketed cycles. Require measured\n  fitness strictly above 120.29, more than 4.6 percent above 115.00.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Move M-operation selection before arithmetic: implement five signed\n      non-restoring remainder updates per existing iteration using captured\n      +D/-D, a 33-bit remainder and final remainder correction; replace\n      three signedness-specific multipliers with one signed 33x33 product\n      whose operand extension bits encode the operation. Preserve the\n      exact two