{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_48", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r7s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-001-r7s0\ntitle: \\\"Flop register file with balanced one-hot operand reads\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 128.30 at 53.914 MHz and 4,202,272 CoreMark cycles.\n  Its worst reported path starts at the register file and returns through\n  execute control to ID/EX. Prior micro_opt attempts concentrated on the\n  divider, recovery payload gating, and M-extension selection. The r6s1\n  divider and dedicated-address improvements now expose operand delivery.\n  A temporary squash-only prototype measured just 53.600 MHz median;\n  its detailed vendor report showed a BSRAM register-file output with\n  2.260 ns clock-to-output followed by ID bypass, EX forwarding and\n  arithmetic/control logic. Localizing storage and making operand reads\n  explicit may shorten several critical execute paths together.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only the physical RTL organization of the 32-by-32 register\n  file. Implement x1 through x31 as independent 32-bit word registers\n  inside constant-index generate blocks, each with synchronous reset\n  and a local write enable w_en && w_addr == its constant index. Do not\n  store x0. Preserve the existing interface and zero added cycles.\n\n  Decode each read address into mutually exclusive per-word selects.\n  Mask each word with its select and combine the 32 leaves, including\n  a constant-zero x0 leaf, with an explicit balanced binary OR tree for\n  each read port. Use statically indexed generate wiring, not a serial\n  procedural OR accumulator or a variable-index memory read. Keep each\n  word as local scalar register storage so the synthesis result uses\n  fabric flip-flops rather than folding ID/EX operand capture into\n  BSRAM read ports. Confirm that mapping in the Gowin resource/netlist\n  report; this is an implementation change, not a synthesis-only timing\n  constraint or a false-path declaration.\n\n  Preserve the existing final read priority exactly: address zero\n  returns zero even on an attempted write to x0; otherwise an enabled\n  same-address write returns w_data combinationally; otherwise return\n  the selected stored word. Reset still clears all architectural\n  registers synchronously, even when a write is requested. Keep both\n  read ports independent and all 32 operand bits. The direct divide\n  completion continues to use this same write-first bypass.\n\n  This is a local storage/read-mux micro-optimization. Keep ID/EX payload\n  and squash behavior, forwarding ownership, branch prediction, ALU and\n  divider arithmetic/latency, memory handshakes and nret=1 unchanged.\n  It aims to replace slow BSRAM launch and associated operand muxing\n  with local flop launch and balanced reads while retaining the existing\n  operand capture boundary. Extra flip-flops, read-address fanout and\n  routing may offset the gain, and multiplication or divider recurrence\n  may become the next bottleneck. Measure the actual mapped circuit.\n\n  Validate all register addresses and both read ports with a scoreboard,\n  all read-address pairs after distinct writes, repeated overwrites,\n  write-first collisions on either/both ports, x0 writes, disabled writes,\n  and reset concurrent with write. Run the existing pipeline suites in\n  real and ALTOPS configurations, especially retained-WB forwarding\n  during dmem stalls and direct divide completion-to-ID bypass. Run\n  unchanged formal, including reg, unique, liveness and instruction\n  checks, and full-RVFI cosimulation with ready and stalled buses.\n  Preserve every decoder, arithmetic, CRC, UART, address-bound, marker\n  and retirement-gap check. Canonical CoreMark cycles should remain\n  exactly 4,202,272 under the same bus-stall sequence.\n\n  Acceptance requires measured fitness strictly above 134.2018, more\n  than 4.6 percent above 128.30, using the median of the existing Gowin\n  placement options. The expected gain is an estimate pending prototype\n  validation; no contract, evaluator, benchmark or other core may change.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the indexed register array with 31 independently enabled\n      scalar word registers, explicit per-port one-hot address decoding,\n      masked word leaves and balanced static OR read trees. Keep x0\n      constant, synchronous reset, synchronous writes and the exact\n      combinational write-first bypass priority. Verify fabric-flop mapping.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing tests and add a deterministic scoreboard covering\n      every read-address pair, repeated writes, both-port bypass collisions,\n      x0 priority, disabled writes and reset/write collision. Compare\n      architectural port values before and after edges without depending\n      on the implementation's internal register names.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r7s0.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated hypothesis is schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
