{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_95", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r7s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-001-r7s0\ntitle: \\\"Register forwarding decisions at ID/EX acceptance\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 128.30 at 53.914 MHz with 4,202,272 CoreMark cycles.\n  Its worst reported path crosses the register-file/execute/control cone.\n  Prior micro_opt attempts focused on arithmetic and recovery logic.\n  Local probes found that removing wide squash fanout, using a flop\n  register file, and flattening operand muxes did not clear the acceptance\n  margin. In particular, the flop-register-file probe exposed a worst\n  path from registered source/destination addresses through forward_unit\n  and the ALU into EX/MEM. Comparing tags and selecting forwarding within\n  EX consumes timing budget before the useful arithmetic begins.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime only the forwarding decision across the existing ID/EX boundary.\n  Compute the two operand forwarding selections while an instruction is\n  in decode and register those selections on the same edge that accepts\n  its ID/EX payload. EX then receives registered 00/01/10 selectors for\n  its existing operand muxes, removing tag comparison and youngest-writer\n  priority logic from the start of the execute datapath. Add no pipeline\n  stage, instruction latency, speculation or extra issue capability.\n\n  The decode comparisons must use the destinations and write ownership\n  that will exist AFTER the accepting edge, not the current EX/MEM and\n  MEM/WB tags. For next EX/MEM, use the current ID/EX destination and\n  EX's ctrl_with_trap.reg_write, suppressed when the current instruction\n  is a divide because that route inserts an EX/MEM bubble even on consume.\n  For next ordinary MEM/WB, use the current EX/MEM destination and MEM's\n  ctrl_with_trap.reg_write. Export these small next-write qualifiers from\n  their owning stages so jump-target and memory-misalignment traps cannot\n  create phantom writers. These qualifiers describe an advancing edge;\n  ID acceptance already excludes a dmem hold. Do not compute either\n  qualifier from the selected arithmetic result or a retirement mux.\n\n  Compare incoming IF/ID instruction source fields against those next\n  destinations. Preserve nonzero-destination guards and EX-over-WB-over-\n  local priority. Register both two-bit selections with the exact ID/EX\n  control priority: reset clears, flush_id clears even when stall_id is\n  asserted, otherwise \"'!stall_id captures, otherwise hold. Keep the\n  existing instruction masking when imem is unavailable. A load-use\n  bubble clears selectors; the dependent instruction accepted one cycle\n  later selects the load entering MEM/WB on that same edge.\n\n  During a dmem hold, ID/EX and EX/MEM are held and ordinary MEM/WB keeps\n  its data, destination and write ownership even after valid clears.\n  Hold the registered selectors as well, and never qualify ownership\n  solely with retirement valid. When a divide waits in ID/EX, selectors\n  may hold while older entries drain: its architectural operands have\n  already been captured on div_request, and waiting/consumption inject\n  an inert EX/MEM bubble. Preserve that launch snapshot discipline.\n  A divide held behind stalled older memory must launch with the correct\n  retained selectors when the memory request finally advances. On direct\n  divide consumption, the next accepted instruction gets the divide\n  value through the unchanged register-file write-first path; the divide\n  must not masquerade as an EX/MEM or ordinary MEM/WB forwarding owner.\n\n  Keep EX operand muxes, register-file implementation, ALU/multiplier/\n  divider arithmetic and latency, branch predictor, squash behavior,\n  memory requests and single-channel RVFI unchanged. This is a local\n  control retiming within the current pipeline. It targets the common\n  forwarding-to-arithmetic path rather than changing benchmark cycles.\n  Risks are next-state ownership mistakes, trap handling, and moving the\n  limiting path into decode or the next-write qualifiers.\n\n  Validate next-cycle selectors against the original combinational rule\n  whenever an ordinary instruction executes and whenever a divide launches.\n  Cover EX/WB simultaneous matches, x0, both source ports, all forwarding\n  source combinations, load-use bubbles, repeated memory stalls with WB\n  valid cleared, flush-plus-hold priority, trap-suppressed writers,\n  consecutive divides and completion-to-ID consumers. Retain all original\n  register consistency, real/ALTOPS arithmetic, startup, ten-cycle liveness,\n  retirement order and exactly-once memory tests. Run unchanged formal\n  and full-RVFI ready/stalled cosimulation, including canonical CoreMark\n  CRCs, UART, address bounds and marker checks.\n\n  Canonical CoreMark should retain exactly 4,202,272 bracketed cycles for\n  the same stall sequence. Measure median Gowin Fmax; acceptance requires\n  fitness strictly above 134.2018, more than 4.6 percent above 128.30.\n  Do not alter any contract, evaluator, benchmark, nret value or other core.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Register the existing two operand-selection encodings on ID/EX\n      acceptance, using incoming decode source addresses and destinations\n      of the next EX/MEM and ordinary MEM/WB entries. Add clock/reset,\n      capture and squash controls with reset/squash priority over hold.\n      Preserve nonzero guards and youngest-writer priority.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire decode source addresses, current ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations,\n      next-stage write qualifiers, clock/reset, !stall_id and flush_id\n      into the registered forwarding unit. Keep normal retained-WB data\n      and direct divide completion routing exactly as before.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose the advancing next-EX/MEM write qualifier from ctrl_with_trap,\n      suppressing it for every divide wait and consume edge. Continue to\n      consume the existing selector encodings and capture divider operands\n      only on launch. Preserve all data-path and retirement behavior.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose the advancing next-ordinary-MEM/WB write qualifier after\n      misalignment trap suppression. Preserve retained data/ownership\n      during holds, memory ports and all existing pipeline fields.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add a cycle-aware forwarding-selector reference check and directed\n      next-state ownership cases, including simultaneous writers, x0,\n      trapped writers, load-use, retained WB under dmem holds, squash,\n      divide launch and direct completion consumers. Retain all existing\n      checks and original startup/retirement-gap bounds.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r7s0.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['category']=='micro_opt' and h['id']=='hyp-20261002-001-r7s0'\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Registered-forwarding hypothesis written and validated')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
