{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_73", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r9s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-001-r9s0\ntitle: \\\"Preselect writeback data feeding a shared signedness-controlled multiplier\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 169.11 iter/s at 71.063 MHz with 4,202,272\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts at ordinary\n  MEM/WB read_data and crosses writeback, forwarding and multiplication\n  before reaching EX/MEM alu_result. Prior micro_opt r8s0 reduced six DSPs\n  to two by sharing the product but remained limited by operand delivery.\n  The current champion has already shortened the divider recurrence.\n  Removing selection before and after multiplication is a focused next\n  step that preserves instruction timing and the current pipeline.\nhypothesis: |\n  Shorten the existing combinational multiply path by selecting ordinary\n  load-versus-ALU writeback data before MEM/WB and selecting multiply\n  signedness before a single shared product. These changes address the\n  two selection costs surrounding the same critical arithmetic resource.\n  Keep all pipeline stages, instruction latencies, forwarding decisions,\n  branch prediction and the nret=1 interface unchanged.\n\n  Add a 32-bit wb_value field to mem_wb_t immediately after read_data.\n  On each advancing MEM edge capture\n  in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result into wb_value, alongside\n  the existing control, destination and data fields. Reset clears it.\n  On hold_wb, retain wb_value exactly like the other ordinary WB payload\n  fields while clearing only valid. Keep the original alu_result and\n  read_data metadata for verification; synthesis may prune fields that\n  no longer feed architectural hardware. Do not add an extra data stage.\n\n  Drive core's normal_wb_data directly from mem_wb_w.wb_value. Make\n  wb_stage use the selected retirement bundle's wb_value directly,\n  preserving its write enable and destination rules. Populate direct\n  divide completion's wb_value with div_result alongside alu_result.\n  Direct divide completion continues to reach RF and write-first ID only;\n  EX forwarding must remain connected to retained ordinary WB data.\n  Never gate this retained data or ownership with retirement valid: an\n  older memory hold clears valid while held EX operands still need it.\n\n  Replace the three real signedness-specific products in alu.sv with\n  one signed 33x33 product. Declare signed [32:0] mul_a and mul_b, and\n  signed [65:0] mul_product. Set\n  mul_a={(op==ALU_MULH || op==ALU_MULHSU) && a[31],a};\n  mul_b={(op==ALU_MULH) && b[31],b};\n  mul_product=mul_a*mul_b.\n  MUL returns mul_product[31:0]. MULH, MULHU and MULHSU all return\n  mul_product[63:32] through the existing case-based ALU output mux.\n  The unused top two bits are intentional. Explicit signed declarations\n  and the 33-bit extensions preserve negative signed operands and the\n  full unsigned 32-bit range. Do not add a post-product sign-correction\n  subtraction, a second product, or a masked-OR ALU output tree.\n  Preserve every RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formula and constant exactly.\n\n  Retain the champion's existing EX forwarding case muxes followed by\n  AUIPC/PC and immediate operand muxes. Retain its combinational divide\n  classification from the registered opcode. Do not add operand-mask\n  registers, a predecoded divide flag, or flatten these muxes as part of\n  this hypothesis. Divider arithmetic, eight busy clocks, launch and\n  completion retention, dedicated effective addresses, memory handshakes,\n  x0, youngest-writer priority, trap-suppressed writers and single-channel\n  retirement remain exactly as before.\n\n  This moves the ordinary writeback mux away from the MEM/WB-to-multiply\n  feedback path, removes selection among three high-half products, and\n  reduces DSP operand fanout. Main risks are moving the limiting path\n  into load extraction before MEM/WB, synthesis rebuilding the writeback\n  mux after the register, or a nearly tied divider/branch path limiting\n  the total gain. Evaluate the actual vendor netlist and median Fmax;\n  resource reduction alone does not establish a fitness improvement.\n\n  Update the packed WB test layout without dropping any existing\n  assertions. Check wb_value against an independent selection of the\n  existing alu_result/read_data fields for every load width and sign,\n  ALU/load alternation, reset, and repeated holds after valid clears.\n  Exercise both operand ports from local, EX and WB sources, simultaneous\n  matches, x0, immediate/AUIPC overrides, load-use bubbles, branch recovery,\n  divide launch behind memory stalls, and direct completion to a younger\n  ID consumer. Keep ten-cycle liveness and exactly-once memory/retirement\n  checks. Extend real multiplication tests using independent full-width\n  integer products over signed extrema, high unsigned values, negative\n  by unsigned pairs, powers of two and seeded random pairs. Keep every\n  existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic and divider-protocol assertion.\n\n  Run unchanged formal gates and real/ALTOPS ALU and pipeline tests,\n  selftest RVFI cosimulation with ready and stalled buses, and canonical\n  CoreMark validation. Standard formal uses ALTOPS and is not proof of\n  real M arithmetic. CoreMark should retain exactly 4,202,272 bracketed\n  cycles for ten iterations under the same stall sequence, all five CRCs,\n  required UART text and address bounds. Acceptance requires fitness\n  strictly above 176.88906 iter/s, more than 4.6 percent above 169.11,\n  corresponding to approximately 74.334 MHz at the unchanged cycle count.\n  Do not change evaluator, benchmark, formal or interface contracts.\n\n  Preliminary exploration is negative evidence about nearby alternatives,\n  not a measured claim for this final circuit: preselected WB with flat\n  EX operand masks and the original multipliers reached 70.146 MHz.\n  Adding the shared multiplier to that flat-mux variant reached 73.606\n  MHz, about 3.6 percent above the champion and below acceptance. It used\n  5,043 logic cells, 1,825 registers, zero LUT-RAM, four BSRAMs and two\n  DSPs; all 53 standard formal checks, 13 ALU/pipeline configurations,\n  selftest and canonical CoreMark passed. Registering divide ownership\n  in that variant made timing worse and is excluded. The proposed final\n  circuit retains the original EX operand muxes and opcode decoding to\n  avoid those additional routing changes. Final measurements must be\n  reproduced by the implementation phase before acceptance.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace three products with one signed 33x33 product selected by\n      operand extension bits. Share its high half across MULH/MULHU/MULHSU\n      through the existing case mux; keep ALTOPS, divider, base operations\n      and every arithmetic latency and handshake unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a selected 32-bit wb_value immediately after read_data in\n      mem_wb_t; retain existing control and verification metadata.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture load_data versus in.alu_result into wb_value on advancing\n      MEM edges, reset it normally and retain it across hold_wb.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive normal_wb_data directly from registered mem_wb_w.wb_value,\n      preserving separation from direct divider retirement arbitration.\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the retirement bundle's wb_value for write data, retaining\n      write enable, destination, validity and architectural x0 behavior.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Populate direct divide completion's wb_value from div_result.\n      Preserve existing EX operand muxes