ection clock. Completed\nstate must remain stable until consumed and reset must cancel it.\nRetain the exact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas, with the same six-iteration\nbusy/done timing as real arithmetic; do not shorten the formal schedule.\n\nKeep EX's existing operand snapshots, older-instruction drainage,\ninert EX/MEM bubbles, held memory requests and exactly-once result\ntransfer. With ready buses, a divide occupies one launch edge, six\niteration edges and one consume edge. Consecutive divides should retire\neight clocks apart, and a preceding load-use bubble should extend the\nrelevant retirement gap to nine clocks. Verify the entire stream,\nincluding branch recovery and startup, against the unchanged ten-cycle\nliveness check; do not infer correctness solely from consecutive divides.\n\nAn isolated prototype changed only this divider schedule and the\nlatency-specific unit-test expectations. Gowin placement options 0/1/2\nmeasured 34.438/34.321/34.321 MHz, giving a 34.321 MHz median versus\n31.417 MHz for the champion. Logic fell from 3,819 to 3,757 cells and\nregisters rose from 885 to 886. The worst path moved outside the\ndivider to register-file/redirect/hazard logic ending at ID/EX. This\nnew bottleneck limits the gain below the ideal one-third speedup from\nreducing eight dependent arithmetic steps to six; keep this hypothesis\nfocused on divider retiming rather than also modifying that path.\n\nThe prototype passed the real and ALTOPS ALU/pipeline test suites and\nfull-RVFI selftest plus all three random programs, both with and without\nbus stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed canonical CRC, completion, UART and\nOOB validation and retained exactly 4,202,272 marker-bracketed cycles\nfor ten iterations. Its measured frequency and cycle count imply\nfitness 81.6725, about 9.25 percent above 74.76. All 53 checks generated\nfrom the unchanged single-retirement formal configuration passed,\nincluding liveness and register consistency. These are prototype\nmeas