{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r5s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Launch-preseeded divider with a five-step feedback path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 96.28 at median Gowin Fmax 40.459 MHz and\n  4,202,272 bracketed CoreMark cycles. After direction-token recovery\n  removed the former redirect bottleneck, the latest worst path runs\n  entirely inside u_ex/u_alu, from divisor_q to remainder_q, with\n  185 cells and 30.5 ns arrival time. Its reusable divider contains six\n  dependent restoring subtract/select steps per clock. Earlier structural\n  retiming from eight to six steps improved fitness, but was then limited\n  by recovery logic. That intervening bottleneck has now been shortened.\n  The failed eight-iteration attempt also establishes that latency must\n  be budgeted against load-use bubbles and the complete retirement stream.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make one structural change: redistribute work across the divider's\n  existing launch and six iteration clocks. Process the first two\n  dividend bits on the launch edge, then reuse five unsigned restoring\n  steps on each of six iteration clocks. This processes exactly\n  2 + 6*5 = 32 bits with unchanged request/busy/done/consume timing.\n  Retain the three-bit counter and completion at iteration index 5.\n  The measured feedback path loses one dependent subtract/select stage\n  and its final-iteration step-2 versus step-6 mux disappears. Do not\n  add an iteration: even though seven iterations might fit some steady-\n  state gaps, the existing first-instruction divide test already uses\n  the complete startup allowance with six.\n\n  Let A and D be the unsigned operand magnitudes already computed at\n  launch, and P=A[31:30]. Compute the initial two-bit quotient Qp and\n  remainder Rp of P divided by D using a small exact predecode. For\n  D=1 use Qp=P,Rp=0; for D=2 use Qp={0,P[1]},Rp={0,P[0]}; for D=3\n  use Qp=(P==3 ? 1 : 0),Rp=(P==3 ? 0 : P). For D>=4 use Qp=0,Rp=P.\n  Classify small divisors only when D[31:2]==0: truncating a large\n  divisor to its low bits would be incorrect. For D=0 use \n…[2491 of 5563 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:665dd26d79edaec5]…\nfor each of the existing six iterations. Remove the final\n      partial-step mux. Preserve all ALTOPS formulas and timing, signed\n      and exception behavior, interface and every other ALU operation.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve every existing six-iteration protocol and arithmetic\n      assertion. Add directed vectors for all four leading dividend\n      pairs, divisor magnitudes 0/1/2/3/4, and large divisors sharing\n      those low bits. Cover both signs, remaining dividend bits and\n      all four operations against the independent integer reference.\n      Retain completion retention, reset and seeded full-width tests.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add forwarded and load-dependent divide cases exercising prefix\n      classification, including magnitude changes across signed inputs.\n      Preserve all current latency/startup/liveness assertions and\n      RVFI operand/order/result, stalled-bus, reset and exactly-once\n      memory-side-effect coverage.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
