{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r6s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r6s1',\n'title':'Retire divides at completion to fund a shorter iterative datapath',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 115.00 at 48.327 MHz with 4,202,272 CoreMark cycles.\nIts five-step restoring-divider feedback remains the worst reported path,\nat 26.574 ns and 156 cells. Prior structural retiming improved frequency,\nbut r1s1 showed that eight iterations followed by ordinary EX/MEM/WB transit\nviolate the ten-cycle retirement limit. The current first-instruction divide\nalso exhausts the startup allowance. Instead of adding latency to that same\nroute, a divide-specific completion-to-retirement route can reclaim the two\ndownstream transit cycles after all older instructions have drained.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Make one structural change: give the blocking divider a direct completion-\nto-retirement route, and spend the reclaimed downstream cycles on eight\niterations of four restoring steps. Ordinary instructions keep their current\npipeline. This couples the shortened divider datapath to the retirement\nroute that makes its latency legal; it is not an independent optimization\nof prediction, memory policy or multiplication.\n\nAt launch capture the forwarded operands, magnitudes, signs, original dividend\nand exception flags as today. Initialize quotient_q to the full unsigned\ndividend magnitude and remainder_q to zero; remove launch-prefix arithmetic.\nReuse four full-width unsigned restoring steps on each of eight busy edges,\nprocessing exactly 32 bits. Keep 33-bit trial remainders, 34-bit differences\nand correct borrow detection. Assert done after iteration index 7. Apply\nsigned result correction from completed registered state, preserving all\nRV32M divide-by-zero, signed-overflow and remainder rules. Preserve matching\neight-iteration request/busy/done/consume behavior under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS,\nincluding all existing stand-in formulas. Reset cancels outstanding work.\n\nWhen a valid divide is done and may advance, present its completed result and\nsaved instruction/operand metadata directly at the existing single-channel\nretirement and register-file write boundary. Do not send a second copy through\nEX/MEM or MEM/WB: EX/MEM must capture an inert bubble on both waiting and\nconsumption cycles. Select normal MEM/WB until the divide completion is valid.\nOlder memory operations still block launch until accepted, and older pipeline\nentries drain during the eight iterations, so normal WB and divide completion\ncannot legitimately overlap. Assert this exclusion in tests and preserve\nolder-first behavior; never silently discard an older valid retirement.\n\nExpose a separate registered-state-derived divider-result output from alu.sv\nfor this completion route. Do not feed the general combinational ALU result\nback through the retirement mux into its own WB forwarding input: that creates\na structural combinational loop even if divide opcode selection masks it.\nUse the captured forwarded rs1/rs2 for divide RVFI, pc_next=pc+4 and zero memory\nmasks/data/address for the divide. Keep completion stable until consumed,\nretire and write exactly once, increment the existing order counter once,\nand release ID/fetch on consumption subject to their other stalls.\n\nThe instruction accepted behind the divide must see its result through the\nexisting write-first register-file bypass on the completion/consumption cycle.\nIf fetch is stalled, the register-file write still completes once and later\nconsumers read the committed value. Keep retained ordinary-WB forwarding\nownership and memory-stall behavior intact; do not qualify held forwarding\nsolely with the retirement pulse. Preserve nret=1, every external port,\ndecoder legality, branch prediction, ordinary ALU/multiply latency and memory\nhandshakes.\n\nThis route is intended to preserve the ten-clock first-divide startup bound\nand the load-use/redirect-to-divide retirement bounds, while consecutive\ndivides retire ten clocks apart instead of eight. Validate the entire\nretirement stream with the unchanged liveness check; do not infer safety from\nsteady-state arithmetic latency. Unit tests must reflect eight busy iterations\nand direct retirement without weakening arithmetic, order, startup or the\nten-cycle liveness checks. Test simultaneous would-be WB/completion ownership,\nall divide consumers, x0, stalled fetch, older stalled loads/stores, reset,\nwrong paths and exactly-once stores/retirement in real and ALTOPS modes.\n\nMeasure canonical stalled CoreMark cycles as well as median Gowin Fmax.\nThe frequency benefit must outweigh any added cycles from consecutive divides;\nCoreMark's dynamic divide frequency must be measured. Acceptance requires\nfitness strictly above 120.29, more than 4.6 percent above 115.00. Run unchanged\nformal and full-RVFI cosim and canonical CRC/UART/address/marker validation.\nThe main risks are new retirement-mux fanout, the remaining multiplier path,\nand a subtle startup/retirement gap or completion-bypass hazard. No checker,\nbenchmark, wrapper, nret declaration or other core may change.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':12,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Use four restoring steps over eight iteration clocks with no launch prefix.\nExpose a separate stable divider-result output from registered divide state,\npreserve exact arithmetic/exception behavior and completion retention, and\nmatch eight-iteration timing in ALTOPS while retaining all formulas.'''\n},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Build a divide-completion retirement bundle from held instruction metadata,\ncaptured forwarded operands and the dedicated divider result. Consume once\nwhen advancing; inject an inert EX/MEM bubble throughout the divide including\ncompletion so it cannot retire twice. Keep older memory blocking and drainage.'''\n},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Merge the mutually exclusive normal MEM/WB and divide-completion bundles at\nWB/RVFI. Drive register-file writes and monotonic retirement order from the\nselected bundle while preserving normal retained-WB forwarding and the\nwrite-first path to the younger instruction. Keep single-channel top-level IO.'''\n},\n{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Retain every exact arithmetic, exception, input-stability, reset and held-result\ncheck; update explicit protocol expectations to eight busy iteration edges.\nTest all four operations with signed extremes and seeded full-width operands.'''\n},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Verify direct retirement, absence of a delayed duplicate, exact original RVFI\noperands/result/order and completion-to-ID bypass. Assert no older valid WB\ncoincides with divide completion, and retain unchanged startup and ten-cycle\nliveness bounds. Cover consecutive divides, load-use and recovery gaps,\nall consumer classes, stalled buses, reset and exactly-once memory requests.'''\n}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=84))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
