{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r.get('outcome'),r.get('fitness'),r.get('cycles'))\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)[:1600])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Early direct-control-flow prediction with a small bimodal table improvement 13.38 4202272\nAdd one instruction-aware direct-control-flow predictor: a 128-entry bimodal\nbranch history table with two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[8:2],\ncombined with early B/J immediate target calculation. Initialize counters to\nweakly not taken. For a legal conditional BRANCH, use the counter MSB to\nchoose between PC+4 and PC+sign_extended_B_immediate. Always predict legal\ndirect JAL to PC+sign_extended_J_immediate. Other instructions, including\nJALR, retain sequential prediction. Fetch already exposes the instruction\ncombinationally to decode, so this needs no new pipeline stage, target cache,\ntarget tags, return stack, or global history. Compute predictions from raw\nimem_data and the PC, never from a flush-generated NOP. Validate BRANCH\nfunct3 before predicting; the authoritative decoder and trap rules remain\nunchanged. Only speculate to word-aligned targets in instruction memory\n[0x00000000, 0x00100000); otherwise predict PC+4 and let EX resolve normally.\n\nCarry the exact predicted_next_pc with the instruction through IF/ID and\nID/EX. Use it for both the accepted fetch's PC update and that instruction's\nmetadata. Recompute the actual next PC in EX from forwarded operands and\nexisting trap rules, including PC+4 on misalignment. Redirect and flush only\nwhen a valid, advancing EX instruction's actual next PC differs from its\nrecorded prediction. Thus a correct taken prediction accepts