{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d.get('outcome'),d.get('fitness'),d.get('cycles'))\n  if 'r2s2' in d['id'] or 'r3s2' in d['id'] or 'r4s2' in d['id']:\n   print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n   print('NOTES', d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Early direct-control-flow prediction with a small bimodal table improvement 13.38 4202272\nhyp-20261002-003-r2s2 Prediction-driven fetch ahead across execute backpressure regression 13.99 3980323\nExtend the existing predictor into a bounded fetch-ahead engine with a\nfour-entry FIFO of predicted instructions. Retain the existing 128x2-bit\nbimodal direction table, legal direct-control-flow predecode, direct-target\ncalculation and EX comparison of actual_next_pc with predicted_next_pc.\nThe single architectural change is to let that predicted fetch stream\nadvance independently of decode backpressure while FIFO space remains.\nThis is a stream queue, with no instruction cache, address-based replay,\nnew direction predictor, return stack, or change to arithmetic latency.\n\nSeparate the bus fetch PC from the instruction presented to ID. Each FIFO\nentry holds the existing if_id_t payload: pc, instr, predicted_next_pc and\nvalid. Compute each prediction from the fetch PC and raw imem_data when\nthat bus response is accepted. Preserve the identical predicted_next_pc\nin the queued payload and fetch-PC transition; never recompute a buffered\ninstruction's prediction after the table changes. On an ordinary accepted\nresponse advance the fetch PC to that prediction, even if decode is held\nby load-use or an older dmem operation. Stop fetching when the FIFO is full.\nNever enqueue an instruction while imemReady is zero.\n\nUse an empty-FIFO combinational bypass so zero-wait instruction delivery\nretains its current latency. ID sees the oldest buffered entry whenever\nthe FIFO is nonempty; otherwise it sees the current bus response only if\nimemReady is high. A ready bus response consumed directly by ID is not\nalso enqueued. If ID cannot accept it, enqueue it once and advance the\nfetch PC. When buffered ID delivery and bus acceptance occur together,\npop the old head and append the new fetched payload in order. Include the\nfull-FIFO simultaneous-pop/push case. A head presented during load-use or\ndmem backpressure must rema\n…[13975 of 17047 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ae629fef16c15944]…\ntation approval;\nrerun correctness gates and median vendor timing on the final RTL.\n\nValidate the token predicate against the old full-PC comparison,\nincluding offset +4,\nzero/backward offsets, misaligned and out-of-range direct targets,\nlegal/reserved control encodings, JALR, and PC arithmetic wraparound.\nExercise recovery together with load-use, imem stalls, older dmem\nstalls and divide completion. Run unchanged formal, real/ALTOPS unit\ntests, full-RVFI cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark validation. Keep\nnret=1, every external port, all CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks and the\nten-cycle ready-bus retirement-gap requirement. Acceptance requires\nmeasured fitness strictly above 85.42682, more than 4.6 percent above\n81.67; at 4,202,272 cycles this needs median Fmax above 35.8987 MHz.\n\nNOTES \nhyp-20261002-003-r5s2 Fixed-head predicted fetch queue with direct-token recovery regression 100.78 3980323\nhyp-20261002-003-r6s2 Three-entry predicted-stream buffer with lossless compact PC tokens regression 120.69 3981775\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
