{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  s=h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r3s2','r4s2','r6s2')): print('\\\\n',d['id'],'\\\\n',s)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 \n Add a four-entry fall-through FIFO to the existing predictor so its fetch\nPC advances independently of decode backpressure whenever an instruction\nresponse is ready and there is queue capacity. Preserve the existing\n128x2-bit BHT, early direct-JAL prediction, direct-target range checks,\nexactly-once EX training and EX misprediction recovery. This is one\npredictor-stream buffering change; leave arithmetic, direction policy,\nmemory semantics and pipeline depth intact.\n\nThe distinguishing implementation is compact prediction metadata. Each\nqueue entry contains the full 32-bit instruction, full 32-bit fetch PC,\nand one frozen use_direct_target bit, rather than a 32-bit predicted PC.\nSet that bit only when the existing legal BRANCH/JAL predecode actually\nselects its aligned, in-range direct target. All other predictions are\nPC+4. Reconstruct the queued instruction's exact predicted_next_pc from\nits saved PC, saved instruction and saved bit at delivery to ID; do not\nconsult the current BHT or reinterpret its direction. This is lossless\nfor the present predictor, which never predicts indirect JALR targets.\nPreserve full-width PC arithmetic and sign extension, including PC+4\nwrap semantics. Retain the existing 32-bit predicted_next_pc in IF/ID\nand ID/EX and the authoritative EX actual-next-PC comparison. Compact\nentries save 124 payload bits across four slots relative to the prior\n96-bit entries. Use per-entry enables and circular read/write pointers,\navoiding a wide shift of every payload on each dequeue. Reset occupancy\nand pointers; unused payload need not be reset if it cannot become valid.\n\nKeep the empty-queue combinational bypass so a ready response can issue\nimmediately without an added stage. With a nonempty queue, present the\noldest saved payload independently of downstream hold/consume signals.\nIf decode consumes a bypassed response, do not also enqueue it. If decode\nis blocked, enqueue an accepted response once and advance the fetch PC\nusing that response's prediction. Permit simulta\n…[13137 of 16209 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a63bae0a97bcfce4]…\ntrap, forward-progress and RVFI semantics.\n\nAn isolated prototype of this compact three-entry design completed the\ncanonical ten-iteration CoreMark run in 3,981,775 bracketed cycles with\nboth bus-stall modes, passing CRC/UART, completion, marker and bounds\nvalidation. Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 47.294/49.622/49.622\nMHz, median 49.622 MHz. This implies fitness about 124.62, 8.37 percent\nabove 115.00. Option zero used 3,756 logic cells and 1,012 registers;\nits worst reported path crossed WB/forwarding/execute to data memory.\nAll eight full-RVFI comparisons for selftest and random1/2/3 passed\nwith ready buses and with combined instruction/data stalls.\n\nThese are prototype measurements, not approval of the eventual RTL.\nPlacement sensitivity remains material: this cycle count requires median\nFmax above 47.89678 MHz. Require all existing correctness gates, canonical\nstalled CoreMark validation and median Gowin timing on the final design;\nfitness must exceed 120.29, more than 4.6 percent above 115.00.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
