{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_67", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-003-r9s2\ntitle: \\\"Pipeline branch-predictor training to isolate execute fanout\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Direct prediction already removes most branch bubbles. Earlier trace\n  replay found only 54,467 conditional misses in 4,202,272 CoreMark cycles,\n  making accuracy alone unlikely to clear the 4.6 percent threshold.\n  Fresh experiments on this champion also reject another stream queue:\n  two entries reached 3,991,298 cycles but only 66.228 MHz median; three\n  entries with registered-capacity admission reached 3,988,312 cycles but\n  only 67.208 MHz. Their saved cycles did not compensate for timing cost.\n  Several near-critical paths run from forwarded EX operands through\n  branch resolution into the BHT counter registers. The current branch\n  result and advancing enable directly drive 128 local counter updates.\n  Isolating that training fanout is a smaller, previously untried predictor\n  change that leaves the instruction delivery datapath intact.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one clock of pipelining to BHT training inside if_stage. Register a\n  nine-bit event consisting of valid, branch_pc[8:2], and branch_taken.\n  Each clock capture valid = branch_update, including zero on cycles\n  without an advancing legal branch. Capture index and outcome only when\n  branch_update is asserted. Reset clears the pending event. Keep the\n  existing EX generation of branch_update: only a valid, legal, nontrapping\n  conditional branch that advances can emit an event. Do not change EX\n  recovery, branch comparisons, operand forwarding or pipeline holds.\n\n  Update each of the existing 128 two-bit counters from the PREVIOUS\n  registered event rather than directly from EX. Keep local per-counter\n  saturation logic, PC indexing, weakly-not-taken reset and the current\n  table read structure. At every edge, consume the old event once and\n  independently capture the next event, permitting one resolved branch\n  per cycle with no backpressure. Pending training must drain even while\n  EX or instruction fetch is held; it represents an already resolved\n  instruction. A recovery must not cancel its legal resolving branch's\n  training. Reset alone cancels pending training and initializes counters.\n\n  The resolving condition then drives one outcome flop, and the EX\n  advancement event drives the small event register, rather than a wide\n  bank of counter enables and state transitions. Counter decode and\n  saturation begin at the new local register boundary. This removes\n  predictor-update fanout from the late forwarded-operand branch path\n  without adding a fetch-stage mux, speculative queue or recovery bubble.\n  Implement the event as scalar fabric flops; do not introduce a RAM,\n  read-modify-write counter port or table-size change.\n\n  Deliberately allow the direction table to be one resolution event older.\n  Do not add an EX-to-fetch or pending-update prediction bypass, which\n  would recreate the timing connection. Fetch still reads pre-edge\n  counters, predicts legal aligned in-range BRANCH/JAL targets using the\n  existing guards, and freezes exactly the token used to advance PC.\n  EX checks that saved token using the unchanged direction-XOR recovery\n  and offset-four exception. Thus training latency may change prediction\n  accuracy, but cannot change architectural next PCs, trap behavior,\n  retirement or memory effects. Sequential JALR prediction is unchanged.\n\n  Preserve the current 32-bit target/address semantics, nret=1, divider\n  protocol, register-file and forwarding implementation, bus handshakes,\n  RVFI and all correctness gates. In particular, neither correct-prediction\n  issue nor misprediction recovery gains an extra pipeline clock.\n\n  This is a timing hypothesis, not a demonstrated fitness improvement.\n  The champion's worst path also crosses writeback and multiplication;\n  that unchanged path may limit any benefit from reduced predictor fanout.\n  Closely spaced branches that alias in the BHT can suffer extra misses\n  from delayed training. Expect a modest Fmax improvement with little\n  cycle change, but confidence is low until vendor timing is measured.\n  At the existing 4,202,272-cycle count, acceptance requires median Fmax\n  above 74.333 MHz; recompute that threshold using the measured cycle\n  count if prediction timing changes it. Require fitness strictly above\n  176.88906 iterations/second, more than 4.6 percent above 169.11.\n\n  Validate an independent cycle-accurate delayed-training table model,\n  existing formal, real/ALTOPS unit tests, ready/stalled full-RVFI cosim\n  and canonical stalled CoreMark. Preserve all canonical CRCs, UART text,\n  address bounds, timing markers and retirement-liveness checks. Measure\n  all three Gowin placement options on the eventual implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a resettable valid/index/outcome event register between existing\n      EX branch-training inputs and the local BHT counter writes. Drain one\n      pending event and accept the next on each edge. Retain all prediction,\n      PC-update, target guards, saturation and counter-reset behavior;\n      introduce no training-to-prediction bypass or fetch queue.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Model the one-clock training delay explicitly, keeping all architectural\n      and saved-token recovery assertions. Test consecutive same-index and\n      different-index events, saturation, pending-event drain during imem,\n      dmem and divide holds, reset cancellation, recovery and no duplicate\n      updates. Adapt exact prediction-sequence expectations against an\n      independent delayed-table model rather than deleting them. Preserve\n      legal-branch-only event checks, wrong-path side-effect checks, RVFI\n      PC/order/operand checks and all existing liveness and arithmetic tests.\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised hypothesis schema valid')\nprint('Exact unchanged-cycle threshold MHz:',176.88906*4202272/1e7)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
