{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r10s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-001-r10s0',\n'title':'Reduce recovery fanout by separating ID/EX payload from bubble control',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 177.89 iter/s at 75.4 MHz with 4,238,604 marked CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path begins at ID/EX rs1_val and ends at a registered forwarding selector through EX and ID logic (19.637 ns, 18 cells). Recovery still synchronously clears the entire wide ID/EX bundle, coupling branch resolution to payload control and routing. The earlier micro_opt r4s0 proposal demonstrated a 17.6 percent gain from separating payload from squash but lost that round to a slightly better alternative; its payload-capture optimization is absent from this champion. The successful r7s0 forwarding retiming explicitly identified next-state control as a possible next bottleneck. Revisit this local fanout optimization on the current MEM-multiply core.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Make one local control-cone change: remove flush from ID/EX payload reset and capture control. Keep all pipeline stages, fetch masking, branch comparators, prediction policy, registered forwarding selectors, arithmetic units, stall priorities and instruction latencies unchanged. This proposal targets recovery fanout, not a predictor or pipeline redesign.\n\nIn id_stage.sv, preserve synchronous reset of the entire bundle to zero. Outside reset, update ctrl and valid separately: flush clears ctrl to zero and valid to zero even when stall is asserted; otherwise \"'!stall captures ctrl_decoded and in.valid; otherwise retain them. Preserve the existing imem-unavailable NOP masking and associated decoded-control behavior exactly. Never rely on validity alone to suppress side effects: memory, hazard and forwarding logic also consume control bits.\n\nCapture all payload fields only when !stall, without flush in their reset or write-enable cone: pc, predicted_next_pc, predicted_direct, direct_is_fallthrough, rs1_val, rs2_val, imm, rd, rs1_addr, rs2_addr and instr. Keep reset initialization. With flush && stall, retain payload while clearing controls and validity. With flush && !stall, capture the incoming payload while clearing controls and validity. Payload on an invalid bubble may therefore be nonzero; it cannot become a forwarding owner, issue memory or division, train prediction, redirect, write back or retire because the original bubble-control discipline is retained. A subsequent hold must not resurrect cleared controls. Preserve all metadata on genuine memory/divide holds and capture matching metadata when the next valid instruction advances.\n\nThe physical intent is to keep late EX recovery away from hundreds of payload bits and reduce routing load and recovery-dependent payload muxing. Keep forward_unit'\"'s existing squash behavior and next-owner rules, including trap-cancelled writes and retained ordinary WB forwarding. Do not add a fetch queue, change the multiplier/divider, alter branch training, gate payload with in.valid, or remove architectural checks. This is solely a local separation of data capture from bubble control at the existing ID/EX boundary.\n\nRetain nret=1, all top-level ports, exact in-order RVFI retirement, legality defaults, alignment traps, memory handshakes and the fixed ten-cycle liveness bound. Extend the ID-stage test to verify the complete expected bundle: cleared ctrl/valid plus held payload for squash with hold, and captured payload with cleared ctrl/valid for squash without hold. Retain the exact all-zero reset assertion. Exercise killed stores, loads, divides and jumps, recovery with imem stalls, and older dmem holds; verify no side effects, duplicate retirement or stale writer ownership. Run lint, real/ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged formal, full-RVFI ready/stalled selftest and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks.\n\nThe expected benefit is higher Fmax without changing the 4,238,604 bracketed CoreMark cycles under the same stall sequence. Require measured median Gowin fitness strictly above 186.07294 iter/s, more than 4.6 percent above 177.89; this requires approximately 78.869 MHz at unchanged cycles. Other control paths or divider arithmetic may limit the result, so measure the full vendor netlist rather than inferring success from fanout alone.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Separate ctrl/valid squash handling from payload capture in the existing ID/EX register. Reset the whole bundle normally. Flush clears only controls and validity, with priority over hold; payload captures on \"'!stall regardless of flush and otherwise holds. Preserve all decode, fetch, prediction and forwarding interfaces.'\"'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain architectural assertions and all-zero reset checks. Update representation-specific ID-stage squash assertions to require ctrl=0 and valid=0 plus exact held/captured payload as appropriate. Cover squash with and without stall, subsequent holds, killed side-effecting instructions, recovery/backpressure overlap and next valid capture; retain full-core RVFI and exactly-once behavior checks.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised hypothesis schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r10s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-001-r10s0',\n'title':'Reduce recovery fanout by separating ID/EX payload from bubble control',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 177.89 iter/s at 75.4 MHz with 4,238,604 marked CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path begins at ID/EX rs1_val and ends at a registered forwarding selector through EX and ID logic (19.637 ns, 18 cells). Recovery still synchronously clears the entire wide ID/EX bundle, coupling branch resolution to payload control and routing. The earlier micro_opt r4s0 proposal demonstrated a 17.6 percent gain from separating payload from squash but lost that round to a slightly better alternative; its payload-capture optimization is absent from this champion. The successful r7s0 forwarding retiming explicitly identified next-state control as a possible next bottleneck. Revisit this local fanout optimization on the current MEM-multiply core.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Make one local control-cone change: remove flush from ID/EX payload reset and capture control. Keep all pipeline stages, fetch masking, branch comparators, prediction policy, registered forwarding selectors, arithmetic units, stall priorities and instruction latencies unchanged. This proposal targets recovery fanout, not a predictor or pipeline redesign.\n\nIn id_stage.sv, preserve synchronous reset of the entire bundle to zero. Outside reset, update ctrl and valid separately: flush clears ctrl to zero and valid to zero even when stall is asserted; otherwise \"'!stall captures ctrl_decoded and in.valid; otherwise retain them. Preserve the existing imem-unavailable NOP masking and associated decoded-control behavior exactly. Never rely on validity alone to suppress side effects: memory, hazard and forwarding logic also consume control bits.\n\nCapture all payload fields only when !stall, without flush in their reset or write-enable cone: pc, predicted_next_pc, predicted_direct, direct_is_fallthrough, rs1_val, rs2_val, imm, rd, rs1_addr, rs2_addr and instr. Keep reset initialization. With flush && stall, retain payload while clearing controls and validity. With flush && !stall, capture the incoming payload while clearing controls and validity. Payload on an invalid bubble may therefore be nonzero; it cannot become a forwarding owner, issue memory or division, train prediction, redirect, write back or retire because the original bubble-control discipline is retained. A subsequent hold must not resurrect cleared controls. Preserve all metadata on genuine memory/divide holds and capture matching metadata when the next valid instruction advances.\n\nThe physical intent is to keep late EX recovery away from hundreds of payload bits and reduce routing load and recovery-dependent payload muxing. Keep forward_unit'\"'s existing squash behavior and next-owner rules, including trap-cancelled writes and retained ordinary WB forwarding. Do not add a fetch queue, change the multiplier/divider, alter branch training, gate payload with in.valid, or remove architectural checks. This is solely a local separation of data capture from bubble control at the existing ID/EX boundary.\n\nRetain nret=1, all top-level ports, exact in-order RVFI retirement, legality defaults, alignment traps, memory handshakes and the fixed ten-cycle liveness bound. Extend the ID-stage test to verify the complete expected bundle: cleared ctrl/valid plus held payload for squash with hold, and captured payload with cleared ctrl/valid for squash without hold. Retain the exact all-zero reset assertion. Exercise killed stores, loads, divides and jumps, recovery with imem stalls, and older dmem holds; verify no side effects, duplicate retirement or stale writer ownership. Run lint, real/ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged formal, full-RVFI ready/stalled selftest and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks.\n\nThe expected benefit is higher Fmax without changing the 4,238,604 bracketed CoreMark cycles under the same stall sequence. Require measured median Gowin fitness strictly above 186.07294 iter/s, more than 4.6 percent above 177.89; this requires approximately 78.869 MHz at unchanged cycles. Other control paths or divider arithmetic may limit the result, so measure the full vendor netlist rather than inferring success from fanout alone.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Separate ctrl/valid squash handling from payload capture in the existing ID/EX register. Reset the whole bundle normally. Flush clears only controls and validity, with priority over hold; payload captures on \"'!stall regardless of flush and otherwise holds. Preserve all decode, fetch, prediction and forwarding interfaces.'\"'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain architectural assertions and all-zero reset checks. Update representation-specific ID-stage squash assertions to require ctrl=0 and valid=0 plus exact held/captured payload as appropriate. Cover squash with and without stall, subsequent holds, killed side-effecting instructions, recovery/backpressure overlap and next valid capture; retain full-core RVFI and exactly-once behavior checks.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised hypothesis schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Revised hypothesis schema valid\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
