{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_48", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r10s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-001-r10s0',\n'title':'Boolean saturating-counter updates to shorten branch outcome fanout',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 177.89 iter/s at 75.4 MHz with 4,238,604 marked CoreMark cycles. With multiplication moved behind EX/MEM and forwarding selectors registered, recovery and training paths now compete with divider timing. Current if_stage implements each of 128 two-bit counter updates with conditional increment/decrement and saturation-dependent holds. This duplicates branch-direction-dependent update control across the entire table. Earlier micro_opt work demonstrated that physical operand/control mapping matters; the recent delayed-training predictor attempt regressed, so retain training timing and change only the local counter logic equations.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Rewrite the existing two-bit saturating-counter next-state equations in if_stage.sv as a small Boolean network, removing conditional arithmetic and saturation-dependent clock enables. Keep the same 128 entries, PC[8:2] indexing, ordinary binary encoding, reset value 01, MSB prediction, read-before-write behavior and same-cycle training. This is a micro_opt of an existing state machine, with no change in prediction policy, capacity, aliasing, training schedule, pipeline boundaries or instruction timing.\n\nFor each entry, let H=counters[i][1], L=counters[i][0] and T=branch_taken. On the existing branch_update && branch_pc[8:2]==i write condition, assign H_next=(H && L) || (T && (H || L)) and L_next=(H && \"'!L) || (T && (!H || L)). These equations implement all eight exact saturating transitions: for T=1, 00->01, 01->10, 10->11, 11->11; for T=0, 00->00, 01->00, 10->01, 11->10. Saturation is represented in data, not as an extra outcome-dependent hold. Retain reset priority and hold both bits when this entry is not selected. Use constant-index generated entry-local logic, not a second variable-index table read or a centralized incrementer.\n\nThe existing branch_update qualifier remains unchanged, including advance, legality and alignment checks. In particular, do not delay training, register branch outcome, eliminate misalignment qualification or suppress valid saturated writes in a way that reintroduces direction-dependent enable logic. The fetch and ID/EX prediction token must continue to sample the original pre-edge counter state. Keep the original branch comparators, direction-token recovery, register-file and forwarding logic, all arithmetic and memory controls unchanged.\n\nThe intended physical gain is less per-entry arithmetic, muxing and clock-enable logic on the high-fanout late branch outcome. Synthesis may rebuild an equivalent network or expose divider/completion timing; the claim requires median vendor timing, not just smaller RTL. Check Gowin'\"'s counter bit data/enable cones and resource count. Preserve nret=1 and every legality, liveness, arithmetic, handshake, retirement and CoreMark invariant.\n\nExhaustively check all four old states and both outcomes against an independent saturating-integer reference, plus reset and no-update holds. Exercise consecutive updates to the same index, different and aliased branch PCs, a fetch concurrent with training the same entry, both saturation endpoints, cold and warmed loops, misaligned/reserved branch suppression and older memory holds. Retain all existing pipeline and prediction assertions. Run lint, real/ALTOPS pipeline tests, unchanged formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI selftest and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks.\n\nThe cycle schedule and predictions must match the champion exactly, retaining 4,238,604 bracketed cycles for the same CoreMark bus-stall sequence. Require median measured fitness strictly above 186.07294 iter/s, more than 4.6 percent above 177.89; approximately 78.869 MHz is required at unchanged cycles.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace only the entry-local saturating increment/decrement/hold update with the exact Boolean H_next and L_next equations. Preserve reset, address-qualified branch_update enable, table shape, binary encoding, same-cycle training, fetch prediction and all PC control.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Extend the IF-stage counter checks with exhaustive four-state/two-outcome transitions using an independent saturating-integer reference, reset and unselected holds. Preserve existing same-index read-before-write, alias, saturation, prediction-token, recovery and stalled full-core assertions.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nfor h in range(2):\n for l in range(2):\n  for t in range(2):\n   n=((h and l) or (t and (h or l)))*2+((h and not l) or (t and (not h or l)))\n   assert n==max(0,min(3,2*h+l+(1 if t else -1)))\nprint('Revised YAML validates; all eight transitions equivalent')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
