{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r10s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Gray-encode existing direction counters to simplify late outcome updates'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 177.89 iter/s at 75.4 MHz with 4,238,604 marked CoreMark cycles. Its worst path crosses execute recovery/control, and local vendor experiments expose branch outcomes feeding counter data and enables as a competing bottleneck. Each of the 128 current two-bit saturating counters uses conditional increment/decrement and saturation holds. Earlier micro_opt successes came from mapping control and storage to FPGA fabric; delayed predictor training recently regressed. Re-encode the same counter state machine to simplify its hardware while preserving every prediction and training edge.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Make one micro_opt change: Gray-encode each existing two-bit bimodal counter while preserving the exact abstract saturating-counter state machine. Keep 128 entries, the same PC[8:2] index, MSB prediction, reset to weak-not-taken, read-before-write sampling, same-cycle training and all external behavior. This is a physical state-encoding optimization, not a new prediction algorithm or a delayed-training design.\n\nMap the original abstract states SN/WN/WT/ST, formerly binary 00/01/10/11, to Gray codes 00/01/11/10. The high bit P remains the prediction direction; the low bit W now indicates a weak state. Reset remains 01. For each constant-index entry, retain the exact existing reset and branch_update && branch_pc[8:2]==i update condition. On that update edge assign P_next = W ? branch_taken : P, and W_next = P XOR branch_taken, using OLD P and W on both right-hand sides. Otherwise hold both bits. These equations give taken transitions 00->01->11->10->10 and not-taken transitions 10->11->01->00->00. Do not use the original numeric increment/decrement operators on the new encoding.\n\nThe next prediction is a three-input mux and the weakness bit is a two-input XOR, removing arithmetic and binary saturation detection from the per-entry update cones. Keep entry-local constant-index storage and the current fetch read mux. Do not add a centralized update read port, extra counter bits, new pipeline registers, a training queue or a new high-fanout decode. The original branch_update qualification stays intact, including execution advance, legality, alignment and older-memory holds. The PC update and ID/EX prediction token continue to sample the same pre-edge prediction as before. Preserve all operand, recovery, forwarding, ALU, divider, memory and RVFI logic.\n\nThis encoding admits a direct state-equivalence argument: encode binary state S as {S[1], S[1] XOR S[0]}; the proposed next-state equations equal encode(saturate(S + (taken ? 1 : -1))) for all eight state/outcome pairs. Reset and unselected holds also correspond, and the predicted MSB is invariant under encoding. Therefore every prediction and update schedule should match the champion for arbitrary outcome/index sequences. Check that relationship with an independent integer-saturation reference; an ISA formal pass alone cannot establish identical prediction performance.\n\nRetain nret=1, all interface ports and every legality, liveness, arithmetic, memory and retirement invariant. Extend test_pipeline.py with IF-stage counter tests that apply all 256 eight-outcome sequences from reset, compare prediction before and after every edge against an ordinary saturating integer counter, and include no-update holds and updates to another entry. Explicitly confirm coverage of all four abstract states and both outcomes. Keep existing alias, read-before-write, guarded-token, saturation, warm-loop, memory-stall and misaligned/reserved-branch tests. Run lint, real/ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged formal, full-RVFI ready/stalled selftest and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker validation.\n\nThe intended gain is shorter and smaller counter-update logic on the late branch-outcome path without any cycle or prediction change. Gowin may optimize the original binary circuit similarly, infer outcome-dependent enables, or expose divider/completion timing; these are risks to measure. Require actual median Gowin fitness strictly above 186.07294 iter/s, more than 4.6 percent above 177.89. At the expected unchanged 4,238,604 CoreMark cycles, this requires approximately 78.869 MHz. Do not count resource savings or an isolated timing path as acceptance.'''\nd['changes'][0]['description']='''Replace the generated counters' conditional binary increment/decrement and saturation holds with Gray encoding SN=00, WN=01, WT=11, ST=10. On the unchanged entry-qualified training edge assign high_next=low ? branch_taken : high and low_next=high XOR branch_taken. Keep reset=01, high-bit prediction, table shape, all PC logic and same-cycle training.'''\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nfor s in range(4):\n for t in range(2):\n  e=s\"'^(s>>1); h=e>>1; l=e&1\n  got=((t if l else h)<<1)|(h''^t)\n  ns=max(0,min(3,s+(1 if t else -1)))\n  assert got==(ns''^(ns>>1))\nprint('\"'Gray-state equivalence holds for all transitions; YAML schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
