{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r11s0.yaml')\nd=dict(id='hyp-20261003-001-r11s0',title='Remove recovery squash from ID/EX payload register control',category='micro_opt',motivation='''The champion scores 195.55 iter/s at 82.887 MHz with 4,238,604 bracketed\nCoreMark cycles. A fresh three-option Gowin run reproduced exactly\n77.823/82.887/82.887 MHz. The median placement's worst paths start at\nordinary WB data, cross forwarded branch comparison and recovery, and\nend at forwarding-selector reset or ID/EX reset. The shared final ID\ncontrol drives 161 loads in that report. Squashing the entire packed\nID/EX bundle places operands, PC, immediate and metadata on this late\ncontrol even though zero validity and zero controls already suppress\nall architectural effects.\nPrior micro_opt r4s0 demonstrated correctness and a 17.6 percent gain\nfor payload/control separation on an earlier core, but lost its round.\nA nearby r10s0 prototype regressed on the previous recovery design, so\nthis is a timing-sensitive hypothesis for the current token-based core,\nnot a claim that removing a mux necessarily improves vendor placement.\n''',hypothesis='''Make one local control-fanout optimization in id_stage.sv: separate\nID/EX payload register updates from validity and decoded-control updates.\nKeep the fetch instruction NOP mux, hazard equations, EX recovery tokens\nand their interpretation, forwarding-unit protocol, predictor policy,\npipeline depth, all arithmetic and every instruction latency unchanged.\n\nFor payload fields pc, predicted_next_pc, predicted_direct,\ndirect_is_fallthrough, branch_prediction_xor, recovery_branch_check,\nrecovery_static_miss, rs1_val, rs2_val, imm, rd, rs1_addr, rs2_addr and\ninstr, retain synchronous reset to zero. Outside reset, capture on\n\"'!stall, without a flush term in either their enable or data. If stall\nis high, retain payload even when flush is high. In particular, a\nload-use bubble may retain stale operands and metadata, and a recovery\nbubble may capture wrong-path data; those payloads never become valid.\n\nControl fields and valid retain exactly the original priority:\nreset OR flush clears ctrl to zero and valid to zero, overriding stall;\notherwise !stall captures ctrl_decoded and in.valid; otherwise hold.\nDo not merely clear valid. Loads, stores, forwarding ownership and late\nresult detection inspect ctrl fields without always checking valid.\nClearing the whole ctrl bundle is essential to preserve these contracts.\nUse disjoint field assignments so the payload and control processes\nnever drive the same bit of the packed register.\n\nDo not alter the forward_unit'\"'s reset/squash/capture behavior. Its\nselectors still clear on squash and hold during dmem/divide stalls.\nStale recovery tokens are harmless only because EX recovery and BHT\ntraining require an advancing valid instruction; preserve those gates\nand clear instruction controls even when payload capture is disabled.\nA following accepted instruction replaces all metadata and controls\non the same edge. Preserve retained ordinary WB forwarding and the\nsingle-retirement, exactly-once memory and ten-cycle liveness rules.\n\nThe intended timing benefit is removing the late redirect-to-squash\nnet from numerous payload reset/data mux inputs, reducing its fanout\nand leaving only actual control/valid state plus forwarding selectors\nas squash consumers. This adds no cycle or state and predicts the same\n4,238,604 CoreMark cycles under the standard bus-stall sequence. It is\nnot permission to change the decoder, prediction accuracy, memory-ready\nhandling, architectural reset, external IO, nret or verification gates.\n\nAdapt only unit-test assumptions about invalid payload being all zero.\nOn flush require ctrl==0 and valid==0, selectors cleared, and the stated\npayload capture/hold rule. Continue checking full defined reset and\nstable held valid instructions. Add directed recovery and load-use\nbubbles with store/load/divide/illegal wrong-path payloads; combine\nimem stalls, older dmem holds, a completing divide, and forwarded branch\noperands. Check no wrong-path memory transaction, register write,\nretirement, predictor training or extra divide wait. Preserve exact RVFI\noperand/PC/order checks and all existing architectural assertions.\n\nValidate final RTL with strict lint, real/ALTOPS pipeline tests,\nunchanged single-channel formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI cosimulation,\nand canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks. Measure all\nthree Gowin options; success requires fitness strictly above 204.5453\niter/s (more than 4.6 percent above 195.55), requiring median Fmax above\n86.698653 MHz if bracketed cycles remain unchanged. Nearly tied fetch,\ndivider and register-file paths and placement sensitivity limit\nconfidence. This proposal does not include the explored fetch-adder,\none-hot-forwarding or divider-prefix rewrites, which all regressed.\n''',expected_impact=dict(fitness_delta_pct=6,confidence='low'),changes=[dict(file='rtl/id_stage.sv',description='Split ID/EX payload registers from ctrl/valid updates. Preserve synchronous reset for every field; capture payload on \"'!stall independently of flush, and retain original reset/flush-over-stall priority for clearing all controls and validity. Keep interfaces and recovery equations unchanged.'\"'),dict(file='test/test_pipeline.py',description='Check the separate payload and control update rules, preserving full reset and held-valid stability. Update only invalid-payload-zero assumptions, and test wrong-path memory/divide/illegal payloads, recovery with stalls, load-use bubbles, cleared selectors, no side effects, exact RVFI and unchanged liveness.')])\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and schema validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
