{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"agent_message","text":"Broad payload separation reached 79.078 MHz, below the champion. I’m narrowing the proposal to remove squash only from the two 32-bit operand registers, retaining the existing control and recovery-metadata behavior. That variant is unmeasured, so the final hypothesis will carry low confidence."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r11s0.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Squash-independent capture of the two ID/EX operand words'\nd['motivation'] += '''\\\\nExploration on the current core found that separating every payload field\nreached only 79.078 MHz on placement option 1 versus 82.887 MHz for the\nchampion; its limiting path moved to divide-completion/write-first operand\ncapture. Narrow the change to the sixty-four operand bits, preserving the\nexisting PC, recovery-token and decoded-control register implementation.\nThis final narrower circuit has not been timed.\\\\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Apply a single local micro-optimization: remove runtime squash from the\nrs1_val and rs2_val registers at the existing ID/EX operand-capture\nboundary. Keep every other ID/EX field on its original update protocol.\nThe goal is to remove sixty-four data-register loads from the late branch\nrecovery-to-flush net, without changing the PC/recovery metadata controls\nor the forwarding protocol that successful prior iterations established.\n\nSeparate the two operand words from the packed register's bulk reset and\ncapture assignments. For rs1_val and rs2_val only, synchronous reset\nstill writes zero; otherwise \"'!stall captures rs1_data and rs2_data;\notherwise hold. Flush must not appear in these two words'\"' reset, enable\nor data expressions. Their capture therefore proceeds on an advancing\nrecovery bubble, while they hold on a load-use bubble where stall and\nflush are both high. No additional registers or pipeline stage are added.\n\nFor ALL remaining fields, preserve the original implementation semantics:\nreset OR flush clears them to zero, even when stall is high; otherwise\n\"'!stall captures the existing decode values; otherwise hold. This includes\nall ctrl bits, valid, rd/source addresses, PC, immediate, instruction,\npredicted_next_pc, predicted_direct, direct_is_fallthrough,\nbranch_prediction_xor, recovery_branch_check and recovery_static_miss.\nUse explicit disjoint field assignments to prevent multiple drivers when\nsplitting the packed register. Do not leave a whole-reg_q assignment in\none process while separately assigning its operand members elsewhere.\n\nA bubble may now contain nonzero or stale operand words, but it has zero\ncontrols, validity and recovery metadata. Clearing the entire ctrl bundle\nremains mandatory because memory, hazards and forwarding sometimes inspect\ncontrols without validity. Never relax suppression of a wrong-path store,\nregister write, divide launch, branch training or retirement. A following\naccepted instruction replaces both operand words alongside its controls\nand metadata on the same edge. Preserve exact reset values, valid-instruction\nhold behavior, retained ordinary WB forwarding, and youngest-writer/x0 rules.\n\nLeave if_stage, hazard_unit, forward_unit, ex_stage, register-file storage\nand write-first bypass, predictor state/training, decoder, all arithmetic,\nretirement and external interfaces unchanged. Forwarding selectors still\nclear on squash and hold during memory/divide stalls. This is only a\nregister-control fanout change, not a fetch queue, prediction-policy change,\nretimed recovery event, or alteration to instruction latency or nret=1.\n\nAdapt test assertions specifically for the two invalid operand fields.\nOn reset the complete bundle remains zero. On squash all fields EXCEPT\nrs1_val and rs2_val remain zero, and both forwarding selectors still clear.\nCheck the two operands against their independent reset/capture/hold rule;\ndo not simply delete bundle assertions. Held valid instructions must retain\nthe complete bundle. Exercise wrong-path stores, loads, divides and illegal\ninstructions; recovery during imem stalls; deferred recovery behind older\ndmem holds; load/multiply-use bubbles; both forwarding sources; and divide\ncompletion feeding an ID operand. Preserve exact RVFI operand/PC/order,\nno-side-effect, exactly-once memory and ten-cycle liveness assertions.\n\nThe final operand-only variant is unmeasured. A broader payload-separation\nprototype passed strict lint but measured 77.844 MHz on placement option 0\nand 79.078 MHz on option 1, below the champion; those numbers are negative\nevidence, not a claimed gain for this proposal. Restricting the change to\noperand words deliberately retains the original metadata reset network and\nits synthesis structure. Prior micro_opt r4s0 supplies correctness and\nfanout-reduction precedent, while the earlier r10s0 payload experiment and\nthis broader trial justify low confidence. Other explored fetch arithmetic,\none-hot forwarding and divider-prefix circuits also regressed and are not\npart of the final proposal. No prototype measurement establishes acceptance.\n\nRun strict lint, real/ALTOPS pipeline tests, unchanged single-channel formal,\nready/stalled full-RVFI cosimulation, and canonical CoreMark validation.\nExpect unchanged prediction decisions and exactly 4,238,604 marker-bracketed\nCoreMark cycles with the standard stalls, CRCs and UART text. Measure all\nthree vendor placement options. Final fitness must exceed 204.5453 iter/s,\nmore than 4.6 percent above 195.55; at unchanged cycles this requires median\nFmax above 86.698653 MHz. Check actual routed fanout and critical paths;\nfetch arithmetic, divide completion or another existing path may cap gains.\n'\"'''\nd['changes'][0]['description']='Separate only rs1_val and rs2_val into disjoint synchronous-reset, \"'!stall-capture operand registers whose data/enable/reset logic does not depend on flush. Keep original reset-or-flush/hold/capture semantics and expressions for every other ID/EX field, including controls, validity and all recovery metadata.'\"'\nd['changes'][1]['description']='Replace whole-bundle-zero-on-squash assumptions with equally explicit field checks: every non-operand field and both forwarding selectors still clear; each operand follows reset/capture/hold independently of flush. Preserve complete-reset and held-valid checks, add wrong-path side-effect/stall/forwarding cases, and retain all architectural RVFI and liveness assertions.'\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent_str(dumper,data):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,represent_str)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-001-r11s0' and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Final hypothesis is schema-valid:',p)\nPY\ngit status --short\ntail -4 cores/bench/generated/hyp11/squash/formal.log\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
