{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261002-001-r7s0','hyp-20261003-002-r10s1','hyp-20261003-003-r10s2']:\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(d.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-001-r7s0\nImplement the register file as 31 independent scalar 32-bit word registers\ninside constant-index generate blocks, each synchronously cleared on\nreset and written only when w_en && w_addr equals its constant index.\nDo not store x0. Decode each read address into per-word one-hot selects,\nmask each word and combine 32 leaves including a zero x0 leaf through\nan explicit balanced binary OR tree. Use static wiring, not a variable\nindexed RAM read or a serial OR accumulator. Preserve both read ports,\nall bits, synchronous reset/write behavior, and final read priority:\nx0 is zero; otherwise an enabled matching write bypasses w_data;\notherwise return the selected stored word. Verify Gowin maps storage to\nfabric flops and keeps operand capture in fabric at ID/EX, removing the\nslow BSRAM launch that otherwise limits the registered-forwarding change.\n\nOptimize operand acquisition at the existing ID/EX boundary by coupling\nfabric register-file reads with registered forwarding decisions.\nCompute the two operand forwarding selections while an instruction is\nin decode and register those selections on the same edge that accepts\nits ID/EX payload. EX then receives registered 00/01/10 selectors for\nits existing operand muxes, removing tag comparison and youngest-writer\npriority logic from the start of the execute datapath. Add no pipeline\nstage, instruction latency, speculation or extra issue capability.\n\nThe decode comparisons must use the destinations and write ownership\nthat will exist AFTER the accepting edge, not the current EX/MEM and\nMEM/WB tags. For next EX/MEM, use the current ID/EX destination and\nEX's ctrl_with_trap.reg_write, suppressed when the current instruction\nis a divide because that route inserts an EX/MEM bubble even on consume.\nFor next ordinary MEM/WB, use the current EX/MEM destination and MEM's\nctrl_with_trap.reg_write. Export these small next-write qualifiers from\ntheir owning stages so jump-target and memory-misalignment traps cannot\ncreate phantom writers. These qualifiers describe a\n…[16753 of 19825 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e3db2f0c5b9c139e]…\nl branch funct3 values, aligned/misaligned\nPCs, offset four, signed offsets, boundary/wrapped targets, holds and\nsquash. All eight full-RVFI selftest/random-program comparisons passed\nacross ready and jointly stalled buses. All 53 unchanged single-channel\nriscv-formal checks passed, including liveness, uniqueness, PC and\nregister consistency. Arithmetic implementation is unchanged.\n\nThese are prototype results, not approval of the eventual implementation.\nThe late JALR target still drives the fetch-PC data input, and direct\nbranch comparisons remain. Extra recovery on a fall-through or trapping\nJALR can cost cycles on other instruction streams. Preserve every gate\nand rerun real/ALTOPS tests, formal, full-RVFI cosim, canonical stalled\nCoreMark and all three vendor placement options on the final RTL.\nFinal fitness must be strictly above 186.07294 iterations/second,\nexceeding 177.89 by more than 4.6 percent. At the measured cycle count\nthis requires median Fmax above 78.868951 MHz; recompute if cycles change.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
