{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_65", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r13s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-001-r13s0\ntitle: \\\"Shared radix-4 barrel shifter with predecoded shift-distance groups\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 195.55 iter/s at 82.887 MHz. Prior micro_opt\n  register-file bypass and branch-comparator rewrites regressed, and\n  recent divider changes expose competing branch and operand-control\n  paths. The ALU still describes separate left, logical-right and\n  arithmetic-right shifts. A shared, explicitly grouped shifter offers\n  an unexplored way to reduce EX selection depth and routing demand\n  without altering instruction latency, prediction or bus-stall behavior.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the combinational SLL/SRL/SRA datapath in alu.sv with a\n  single right-shift network using two four-way stages and one two-way\n  stage. Share it between left and right shifts through bit reversal.\n  This is one local arithmetic-mapping optimization, with no pipeline\n  register, new instruction, forwarding change or divider modification.\n\n  Let left=(op==ALU_SLL), fill=(op==ALU_SRA && a[31]), and\n  X=left ? reverse_bits(a) : a. Reverse by constant wire permutation,\n  not a dynamic index or shift. Decode b[1:0] into four exclusive\n  enables for shifts of 0,1,2,3 bits, and b[3:2] into four exclusive\n  enables for shifts of 0,4,8,12 bits. These small decodes evaluate in\n  parallel with the operand-source selection.\n\n  Stage one selects X, {fill,X[31:1]}, {2{fill},X[31:2]}, or\n  {3{fill},X[31:3]}. Stage two selects its input unchanged or shifted\n  right by 4,8,12 bits with the corresponding replicated fill bits.\n  Stage three uses b[4] to select unchanged data or a right shift by\n  sixteen bits with sixteen fill bits. All stage vectors remain 32 bits.\n  Use exclusive masks and balanced ORs for the four-way stages, exposing\n  parallel constant-wired candidates to Gowin instead of a priority\n  if/else chain. Do not infer a ROM or use variable shift operators\n  inside the grouped network. Select the final result directly for\n  SRL/SRA, and reverse its bits for SLL. All three ALU case arms return\n  this one result; preserve every other operation and default arm.\n\n  Repeated fill insertion implements arithmetic right shift exactly;\n  logical-right and left shifts use zero fill. Only b[4:0] controls the\n  distance, including register shifts whose upper b bits are nonzero.\n  Distance zero must return a for every shift operation, including\n  negative SRA inputs. The construction covers all distances 0..31,\n  and reversing both sides of a logical right shift implements SLL\n  modulo 32 bits. Preserve the existing standalone ALU interface and\n  both real and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS configurations.\n\n  The intended benefit comes from sharing the directional shift hardware\n  and expressing the five shift-amount decisions as three grouped data\n  stages. Constant wiring and small parallel distance decodes may map\n  more efficiently to Gowin LUT/mux resources. This is an implementation\n  hypothesis: four-way selection is not necessarily one physical LUT\n  level. Extra reversal-selection muxes and control fanout can erase the\n  gain, and existing branch/divider paths can still set Fmax. Confirm the\n  actual mapped depth and resource use rather than assuming fewer RTL\n  stages imply faster hardware. The expected gain is low confidence.\n\n  Keep the existing ALU operand forwarding, EX/MEM and MEM/WB boundaries,\n  multiply and divide implementations and protocols, branch predictor,\n  register-file storage, memory interfaces and nret=1 unchanged. Preserve\n  all decoder legality, trap, RVFI ordering and liveness checks. Identical\n  instruction results and latencies should preserve exactly 4,238,604\n  marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles under the standard stall sequence.\n\n  Prove combinational equivalence of all three shift results against the\n  original native expressions for arbitrary a and b. Test all 32 shift\n  distances with zero, all ones, signed extrema, alternating bit patterns,\n  walking ones/zeros and deterministic random operands. Vary b[31:5] to\n  verify masking, and compare SRA with an independent signed-integer\n  reference. Retain all existing arithmetic, real M-extension and ALTOPS\n  checks. Run unchanged pipeline tests, formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI\n  cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks.\n\n  Measure all three vendor placement options and actual CoreMark cycles.\n  Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 204.5453 iter/s, more than\n  4.6 percent above 195.55; at unchanged cycles median Fmax must exceed\n  approximately 86.699 MHz. No measured improvement is claimed for this\n  grouped shifter proposal. Earlier local branch, divider and control\n  prototypes explored in this session remained below the champion and\n  are excluded from the final change.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace only SLL/SRL/SRA with one bit-reversed shared right shifter,\n      two predecoded four-way constant-shift stages and a final sixteen-bit\n      stage. Use exact sign/zero fill and balanced masked selection, keeping\n      all other operations, interfaces and sequential arithmetic unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Add independent reference coverage for all shift distances, sign\n      boundaries, walking patterns, random operands and ignored high shamt\n      bits. Prove or symbolically check shift equivalence while retaining\n      every existing arithmetic and divider-protocol assertion.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom jsonschema import validate\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r13s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid:',d['id'],d['category'])\nPY\ncat cores/bench/generated/research_r13s0/shared_shift/timing.log\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
