ess_ch0.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (improvement, +10.40%): With combinational IF/ID, a 128-entry bimodal BHT and early JAL targets improve CoreMark by eliminating the single issue bubble on correctly predicted transfers, using EX comparison of actual_next_pc against the latched predicted_next_pc to flush only on misprediction.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (improvement, +458.74%): Reusing eight restoring DIV/REM\n…[5516 of 8588 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8f636853a11d45be]…\nmax from 82.887 to 73.335 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.90%): A fully registered two-entry fetch queue removes the raw-imem decode path but leaves EX-to-fetch-PC recovery and count-to-hazard-to-head feedback near critical, so its 3.51% CoreMark cycle savings are outweighed by median Fmax falling from 82.887 to 77.652 MHz.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r13s1 (regression, -10.58%): Width-specialized radix-8/narrow radix-4 division shortens arithmetic feedback but leaves WB-to-branch-resolution-to-BHT-counter timing critical, regressing median Fmax from 82.887 to 74.120 MHz with CoreMark unchanged at 4,238,604 cycles.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r13s2 (regression, -17.02%): Moving direct-target selection to a 32-entry PC-only cache removes live-imem arithmetic but puts full-tag lookup and signature validation on the combinational IF-to-ID/EX recovery_static_miss path, lowering median Fmax from 82.887 to 69.053 MHz despite only 0.40% more CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
