{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r14s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Compute effective addresses before forwarding selection'\nd['motivation']='''Recent r11s0 and r13s0 micro-optimization timing paths begin at registered forwarding selectors and traverse EX into the fetch PC. The champion unconditionally recovers legal JALRs, removing the forwarded target from recovery enable, but its target data still passes through the rs1 forwarding mux and then a full-width addition. Memory address generation uses the same operands. Reordering this mux and addition can shorten the late-selector path while preserving the exact instruction schedule. Prior branch-comparator and shifter replacements regressed, so this proposal targets only address generation.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace EX's mux-then-add base-plus-immediate address calculation with three parallel additions followed by the existing binary forwarding selection. Define local_sum = in.rs1_val + in.imm, ex_sum = fwd_ex_mem + in.imm, and wb_sum = fwd_mem_wb + in.imm as explicitly 32-bit modulo-2\"'^32 sums. Select address_sum with the unchanged fwd_rs1_sel encoding: 1 chooses ex_sum, 2 chooses wb_sum, and every other encoding chooses local_sum. Use this selected word for both effective_addr and jalr_sum. For JALR clear bit zero only after the full addition; preserve the existing target alignment, trap suppression, fall-through recovery and unconditional legal-JALR recovery rules.\n\nThis is one local distributive transformation of the address datapath, trading two extra adders for removing the source-select dependency before a long carry chain. Do not modify the raw rs1/rs2 forwarding muxes, their encoding, source comparisons or priority. Those raw operands still feed the general ALU, branch comparison, store data, multiplication, divider launch and RVFI. Keep the PC-relative branch/JAL adder and every existing final target mux unchanged. Do not flatten ALU operand selection, duplicate branch comparators, retime recovery or add a pipeline stage.\n\nAll three bases already come from physical registers. Parallel sums must use the retained ordinary MEM/WB result, never the direct divide-completion retirement mux. ID/EX immediates and forwarding selectors retain the existing reset, squash and hold behavior. A stalled older memory request must keep the registered EX/MEM effective address and store payload unchanged. Trap-suppressed writers, youngest-writer priority and x0 behavior remain owned by the existing forwarding logic. On divide completion, younger decode consumers still obtain their operand through register-file write-first bypass. No new forwarding source or availability rule is introduced.\n\nMathematically, select(A,B,C,s)+I equals select(A+I,B+I,C+I,s) modulo 2''^32. Preserve the default selection for binary value 3 as well as all reachable 0/1/2 values. Do not truncate bases or immediates to the benchmark address range; negative offsets, carries from bit zero into bit one, wraparound and full-width JALR values must remain exact. Effective addresses stay byte addresses; MEM retains all alignment checks, lane masks and side-effect gating.\n\nThe expected gain is a shorter registered-selector-to-JALR-PC and selector-to-EX/MEM-address path without changing CoreMark iterations/cycle. Extra adders, immediate fanout, placement or synthesis factoring the expressions back into one adder may erase the benefit. Branch, fetch or divider paths may instead limit Fmax. Inspect the mapped cone and all-placement median timing rather than assuming an algebraic rewrite is faster. Confidence is low until vendor measurement.\n\nProve the address transformation combinationally for arbitrary three 32-bit bases, immediate and all four selector encodings. Test memory and JALR users with local, EX and retained-WB operands, both owners matching, x0, load/multiply-use bubbles and memory holds after WB valid clears. Exercise signed offsets, carry boundaries, wraparound, all low-bit alignments, JALR bit-zero clearing, misaligned trap/link suppression, target equal to PC+4, and wrong-path cancellation. Preserve every existing arithmetic, operand, memory, recovery, RVFI and liveness assertion.\n\nRun strict lint, real and ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged single-channel formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks. Preserve nret=1 and all external ports and contracts. Results and latencies should keep 4,238,604 bracketed CoreMark cycles under the standard stall sequence. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s, over 4.6 percent above 195.55; at unchanged cycles median Gowin Fmax must exceed approximately 86.699 MHz. No measured improvement is claimed yet.\n\nAn earlier independent probe in this proposal session changed only forwarding to registered one-hot masks. It passed ten pipeline test configurations but regressed median Gowin Fmax to 63.286 MHz. That encoding change is rejected and excluded from this hypothesis; restore the champion'\"'s original binary selectors and muxes before implementing the address transformation.'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Compute three parallel 32-bit sums of the immediate with local rs1, EX/MEM and retained ordinary MEM/WB. Select the sum with the unchanged binary rs1 forwarding selector and use it for effective_addr and jalr_sum. Preserve raw operand forwarding, all branch/JALR trap and recovery logic, pipeline registers, arithmetic and metadata.'},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add independent mux-plus-add reference coverage for all address-source selections, carry/wrap and alignment cases. Exercise JALR and memory consumers with forwarding, repeated memory holds, trap suppression and wrong-path cancellation while retaining all existing architectural, real/ALTOPS, RVFI and liveness assertions.'}]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=82))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r14s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Compute effective addresses before forwarding selection'\nd['motivation']='''Recent r11s0 and r13s0 micro-optimization timing paths begin at registered forwarding selectors and traverse EX into the fetch PC. The champion unconditionally recovers legal JALRs, removing the forwarded target from recovery enable, but its target data still passes through the rs1 forwarding mux and then a full-width addition. Memory address generation uses the same operands. Reordering this mux and addition can shorten the late-selector path while preserving the exact instruction schedule. Prior branch-comparator and shifter replacements regressed, so this proposal targets only address generation.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace EX's mux-then-add base-plus-immediate address calculation with three parallel additions followed by the existing binary forwarding selection. Define local_sum = in.rs1_val + in.imm, ex_sum = fwd_ex_mem + in.imm, and wb_sum = fwd_mem_wb + in.imm as explicitly 32-bit modulo-2\"'^32 sums. Select address_sum with the unchanged fwd_rs1_sel encoding: 1 chooses ex_sum, 2 chooses wb_sum, and every other encoding chooses local_sum. Use this selected word for both effective_addr and jalr_sum. For JALR clear bit zero only after the full addition; preserve the existing target alignment, trap suppression, fall-through recovery and unconditional legal-JALR recovery rules.\n\nThis is one local distributive transformation of the address datapath, trading two extra adders for removing the source-select dependency before a long carry chain. Do not modify the raw rs1/rs2 forwarding muxes, their encoding, source comparisons or priority. Those raw operands still feed the general ALU, branch comparison, store data, multiplication, divider launch and RVFI. Keep the PC-relative branch/JAL adder and every existing final target mux unchanged. Do not flatten ALU operand selection, duplicate branch comparators, retime recovery or add a pipeline stage.\n\nAll three bases already come from physical registers. Parallel sums must use the retained ordinary MEM/WB result, never the direct divide-completion retirement mux. ID/EX immediates and forwarding selectors retain the existing reset, squash and hold behavior. A stalled older memory request must keep the registered EX/MEM effective address and store payload unchanged. Trap-suppressed writers, youngest-writer priority and x0 behavior remain owned by the existing forwarding logic. On divide completion, younger decode consumers still obtain their operand through register-file write-first bypass. No new forwarding source or availability rule is introduced.\n\nMathematically, select(A,B,C,s)+I equals select(A+I,B+I,C+I,s) modulo 2''^32. Preserve the default selection for binary value 3 as well as all reachable 0/1/2 values. Do not truncate bases or immediates to the benchmark address range; negative offsets, carries from bit zero into bit one, wraparound and full-width JALR values must remain exact. Effective addresses stay byte addresses; MEM retains all alignment checks, lane masks and side-effect gating.\n\nThe expected gain is a shorter registered-selector-to-JALR-PC and selector-to-EX/MEM-address path without changing CoreMark iterations/cycle. Extra adders, immediate fanout, placement or synthesis factoring the expressions back into one adder may erase the benefit. Branch, fetch or divider paths may instead limit Fmax. Inspect the mapped cone and all-placement median timing rather than assuming an algebraic rewrite is faster. Confidence is low until vendor measurement.\n\nProve the address transformation combinationally for arbitrary three 32-bit bases, immediate and all four selector encodings. Test memory and JALR users with local, EX and retained-WB operands, both owners matching, x0, load/multiply-use bubbles and memory holds after WB valid clears. Exercise signed offsets, carry boundaries, wraparound, all low-bit alignments, JALR bit-zero clearing, misaligned trap/link suppression, target equal to PC+4, and wrong-path cancellation. Preserve every existing arithmetic, operand, memory, recovery, RVFI and liveness assertion.\n\nRun strict lint, real and ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged single-channel formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks. Preserve nret=1 and all external ports and contracts. Results and latencies should keep 4,238,604 bracketed CoreMark cycles under the standard stall sequence. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s, over 4.6 percent above 195.55; at unchanged cycles median Gowin Fmax must exceed approximately 86.699 MHz. No measured improvement is claimed yet.\n\nAn earlier independent probe in this proposal session changed only forwarding to registered one-hot masks. It passed ten pipeline test configurations but regressed median Gowin Fmax to 63.286 MHz. That encoding change is rejected and excluded from this hypothesis; restore the champion'\"'s original binary selectors and muxes before implementing the address transformation.'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Compute three parallel 32-bit sums of the immediate with local rs1, EX/MEM and retained ordinary MEM/WB. Select the sum with the unchanged binary rs1 forwarding selector and use it for effective_addr and jalr_sum. Preserve raw operand forwarding, all branch/JALR trap and recovery logic, pipeline registers, arithmetic and metadata.'},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add independent mux-plus-add reference coverage for all address-source selections, carry/wrap and alignment cases. Exercise JALR and memory consumers with forwarding, repeated memory holds, trap suppression and wrong-path cancellation while retaining all existing architectural, real/ALTOPS, RVFI and liveness assertions.'}]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=82))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"set -e\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path('cores/bench')\nbackup=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/92f133a1/46322b65/r14-forward-backup')\nfor rel in ['rtl/core.sv','rtl/ex_stage.sv','rtl/forward_unit.sv','test/test_pipeline.py']:\n (root/rel).write_bytes((backup/Path(rel).name).read_bytes())\np=root/'rtl/ex_stage.sv'\ns=p.read_text()\ns=s.replace('  logic [31:0] effective_addr;', '''  logic [31:0] effective_addr;\n  logic [31:0] address_sum, local_sum, ex_sum, wb_sum;\n  assign local_sum = in.rs1_val + in.imm;\n  assign ex_sum = fwd_ex_mem + in.imm;\n  assign wb_sum = fwd_mem_wb + in.imm;\n  always_comb begin\n    case (fwd_rs1_sel)\n      2'd1: address_sum = ex_sum;\n      2'd2: address_sum = wb_sum;\n      default: address_sum = local_sum;\n    endcase\n  end''')\ns=s.replace('  assign effective_addr = rs1 + in.imm;', '  assign effective_addr = address_sum;')\ns=s.replace('    jalr_sum    = rs1 + in.imm;', '    jalr_sum    = address_sum;')\np.write_text(s)\nPY\nmake lint TARGET=bench\npython3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options > /srv/hwebench/tmp/92f133a1/46322b65/r14-address-timing.log 2>&1\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
