{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json; git status --short; rg -n '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20261003-003-r10s2|hyp-20261002-003-r8s2|hyp-20261002-003-r5s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Budget iterative DIV/REM against the full retirement gap: eight iteration clocks plus launch/consume exhaust the 10-cycle RVFI liveness allowance, so a preceding load-use bubble stretches the gap to 11 cycles and fails liveness_ch0.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (improvement, +10.40%): With combinational IF/ID, a 128-entry bimodal BHT and early JAL targets improve CoreMark by eliminating the single issue bubble on correctly predicted transfers, using EX comparison of actual_next_pc against the latched predicted_next_pc to flush only on misprediction.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (improvement, +458.74%): Reusing eight restoring DIV/REM steps over four clocks shortens the combinational ALU path enough to outweigh added divide stalls, while launch/consume and a preceding load-use bubble still fit the 10-cycle retirement limit.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r3s1 (improvement, +9.24%): Reducing the shared DIV/REM chain from eight to six restoring steps per clock (five six-bit iterations plus a final two-bit iteration) improves Fmax without changing CoreMark's 4,202,272 cycles, but shifts the critical path to register-file/redirect/hazard logic ending at ID/EX, limiting further gains from divider retiming alone.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r4s0 (regression, +17.60%): Separating IF/ID and ID/EX payloads from squash control shortens the recovery-to-decode/load-use path without extra CoreMark cycles, but requires clearing side-effect controls as well as valid and qualifying raw-source load-use checks with imem_ready.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r4s2 (improvement, +17.89%): Capturing the guarded BRANCH/JAL target-selection bit lets EX recover via direction XOR (except offset +4) instead of a selected 32-bit next-PC comparison, shortening the redirect path without changing CoreMark cycles while JALR keeps a separate full-width comparison.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r5s0 (regression, +17.50%): Registering 34-bit 3D at launch lets three radix-4 steps with par\n…[190715 of 193787 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1f139472632ea3a4]…\nJALR target arithmetic still drives the fetch-PC data input. The deliberate\\nrecovery bubble for a sequential or trapping JALR can cost cycles on instruction\\nstreams other than this CoreMark workload. Placement results are sensitive to\\nequivalent combinational factoring; the numbers above are measurements of the\\nfinal RTL, not the supplied prototype's results.\\n\\nFinal RTL fingerprint (`sha256sum cores/bench/rtl/*.sv | sha256sum`):\\n`f12aac79ecd272c2c4d79f76861efe153b88470eea91f3dfda2b9d5c19ae9f43`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T23:28:27.455958Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r10s2 (improvement, +9.93%): Predecoding direct recovery into ID/EX tokens and unconditionally recovering legal JALRs lets squash and speculative next_ex_mem_w_en bypass forwarded target arithmetic/alignment, improving Fmax with unchanged CoreMark cycles; the owner hint remains safe only when advancing JALRs clear younger ID/EX controls and forwarding selectors while held JALRs disable capture.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
