{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r11s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r11s1',\n'title':'Replace cascaded divide digits with one parallel radix-16 step',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz with 4,238,604 marked CoreMark cycles. Structural history r5s0/r8s1 demonstrated the value of parallel digit trials and overlapping divisor preparation with the narrow first nibble. The current divider nevertheless cascades two wide radix-4 subtract/select networks on each of seven iteration clocks. The r10s1 timing report and exploratory timing in this slot expose that recurrence as a near-critical limit. The current approximately 5,048 LUT4s leave area headroom for replacing serial arithmetic depth with parallel hardware. CoreMark's measured interval contains no division, so a clock improvement translates directly to fitness if the existing schedule is preserved.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Make one structural change to the shared real DIV/REM datapath: replace the two cascaded radix-4 digits with one radix-16 digit selected from fifteen parallel divisor trials. Keep exactly the existing request, eight busy clocks, done/consume protocol and direct divide retirement. Preserve the four narrow restoring prefix steps on busy iteration zero. During that same prefix edge prepare and register 36-bit multiples k*D for k=1..15 from the already registered unsigned divisor. The seven remaining clocks each consume one four-bit digit; four prefix bits plus seven nibbles still cover all 32 dividend bits.\n\nUse full 36-bit unsigned multiples so 15*0xffffffff is represented without overflow. Shifts supply power-of-two multiples. Generate other multiples from constant shifts with one carry-propagate add/subtract where possible: 3D=2D+D, 5D=4D+D, 7D=8D-D, 9D=8D+D, 15D=16D-D, and even multiples can share shifted odd results or equivalent constant expressions. For 11D=8D+2D+D and 13D=8D+4D+D, explicitly compress the three terms into sum=X XOR Y XOR Z and carry=((X AND Y) OR (X AND Z) OR (Y AND Z))<<1, followed by one 36-bit sum+carry. This avoids creating a new two-adder preparation bottleneck. No multiply operator, table ROM or variable-index RAM belongs in divisor preparation. Let synthesis merge identical shifted register bits where possible.\n\nOn each later busy cycle form the 36-bit trial T={remainder_q,quotient_q[31:28]}. In parallel compute fifteen 37-bit differences diff[k]={0,T}-{0,multiple_q[k]}. Define ge[0]=1 and ge[k]=NOT diff[k][36]. For k=0..14, select[k]=ge[k] AND NOT ge[k+1]; select[15]=ge[15]. Select the low 32-bit remainder using masked terms and an OR reduction: T for digit zero, otherwise diff[k]. Form the four quotient bits as OR reductions over the constant digit encodings masked by select[k], then append that nibble to quotient_q[27:0]. Express balanced reductions or synthesis-friendly bitwise ORs, not a priority chain, dynamic indexed result mux, second subtraction or serial comparison of fifteen candidates. For a nonzero divisor the selected remainder is less than D and fits 32 bits. Zero divisors retain the existing explicit architectural result override.\n\nRetain the launch-time unsigned magnitude conversion, captured dividend and signs, quotient/remainder choice, final sign correction, divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics exactly. Do not change multiplication, the ordinary ALU result mux, forwarding, fetch/decode topology, branch prediction, architectural completion ownership, memory interfaces or nret=1. Preserve the existing ALTOPS equations and identical eight-cycle handshake; only the real-arithmetic branch changes. Both arithmetic and control paths must retain reset, completion retention and exactly-once consumption behavior.\n\nThe intended benefit is one wide subtract-and-select depth per four dividend bits instead of two. The cost is fifteen trial subtractors instead of six, additional constant-multiple storage, and a wider one-hot selection tree. Expect a moderate area increase within the 10,000 LUT4 target. Divisor-preparation timing, selection fanout, routing congestion, and the unchanged IF-to-ID recovery-token path can limit the benefit. This is a single divider topology proposal, not a combination with the fetch-buffer or retirement-control experiments. Those exploratory alternatives did not clear acceptance and are excluded.\n\nValidate every real M-extension operation with the unchanged exact arithmetic tests and additional independent randomized quotient/remainder checks, particularly large unsigned divisors, signed extremes and trial values around each digit boundary k*D. Exercise divisor values whose multiples need all 36 bits. Check the recurrence invariant and one-hot digit selection for nonzero divisors against an independent arithmetic reference. Preserve ALTOPS testing, all standard formal checks including the ten-cycle retirement liveness bound, and full-RVFI ready/stalled cosimulation. Keep canonical CoreMark CRCs, UART validation, address bounds and marker-bracketed timing unchanged. Real arithmetic must be checked independently because standard formal uses ALTOPS; do not claim that gate proves the new recurrence.\n\nMeasure all three vendor placement options and actual marked CoreMark cycles. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s, more than 4.6 percent above 195.55. If the expected 4,238,604-cycle interval is retained, median Fmax must exceed 86.698653 MHz. The expected gain below is a hypothesis, not a measured claim; final implementation must satisfy the unchanged gates.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':9,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Replace only the real divider's two-stage radix-4 recurrence with fifteen parallel radix-16 trial subtractors, borrow-derived one-hot digit selection and masked OR reductions. Prepare full-width divisor multiples during the existing first-nibble prefix iteration, using shallow constant arithmetic and carry-save compression for three-term multiples. Preserve the eight iteration clocks, all corner cases, launch/consume protocol, ALTOPS behavior and standalone ALU interface.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Retain every existing exact arithmetic assertion and add independent randomized real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU checks across large divisors, signed extrema, powers of two and radix-16 digit boundaries. Verify full-width multiples and recurrence/one-hot invariants where observable, reset, eight-clock completion, retained done/results, and back-to-back operations. Do not replace real arithmetic checks with ALTOPS checks.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain architectural and timing assertions. Add directed load-to-divide, consecutive-divide and recovered-divide programs using arithmetic vectors that exercise upper quotient digits and 36-bit divisor multiples; check exact RVFI operands/results/order, stable older memory requests, correct consumers and the existing liveness bound under ready and stalled buses.'''}]\n}\nclass LiteralDumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef string(dumper,value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value or len(value)>160 else None)\nLiteralDumper.add_representer(str,string)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=LiteralDumper,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('VALID',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
