{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r12s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Isolate JALR recovery behind dedicated target and event registers\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz. Its r10s2 recovery\n  tokens removed forwarded JALR arithmetic from recovery enable, but the\n  forwarded base-plus-immediate and target-alignment mux still drive the\n  fetch-PC data input. Recent timing paths run from forwarding selectors\n  through EX to the fetch PC. Structural r10s1 established that registered\n  recovery is feasible and costs about 1.6 percent more CoreMark cycles\n  when applied to all transfers. The proposed narrower boundary targets\n  indirect jumps while preserving the champion's direct-branch recovery.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make one structural change: complete JALR recovery through dedicated\n  registered target and pending-event state. Keep conditional branches and\n  direct JAL recovery in their existing EX cycle. Partition the fetch-PC\n  recovery data path so live forwarded JALR arithmetic cannot remain in\n  the fast direct-transfer target cone. This is selective control-transfer\n  retiming, with one additional recovery cycle only for legal JALRs.\n\n  In ex_stage.sv add indirect_pending_q and indirect_target_q. When a\n  valid legal JALR advances without older dmem backpressure or an older\n  pending indirect recovery, capture its actual architectural target:\n  sum = forwarded_rs1 + immediate; aligned target = {sum[31:1],1'b0};\n  if sum[1] is one, capture PC+4 instead and preserve the existing trap\n  and suppressed link write. Capture directly from these JALR expressions,\n  not from a shared branch/JAL/JALR actual_next_pc mux. Use separate scalar\n  state outside the packed EX/MEM register. The pending bit itself proves\n  valid ownership; it must only be set by that accepted legal JALR.\n\n  Define late_redirect = indirect_pending_q AND NOT reset AND NOT stall.\n  Reset clears pending and target. While stall is asserted retain both.\n  On an unstalled edge pending becomes the new accepted-JALR event, or\n  zero if there is none; target updates only for a new accepted JALR.\n  Every late_redirect edge consumes the old event exactly once. Keep the\n  original JALR instruction's architectural EX/MEM and MEM/WB progress,\n  RVFI operands, link value, trap and actual pc_next intact.\n\n  Gate advance and divider launch with NOT late_redirect. Define divider\n  execute_wait with NOT late_redirect too, so a wrong-path younger divide\n  cannot block the older recovery. When EX/MEM is not stalled, insert a\n  fully inert EX/MEM bubble on late_redirect, clearing controls as well\n  as validity. The older recovering JALR still transfers normally to WB.\n  Hazard control sees the recovery and clears the younger ID/EX bundle\n  and both forwarding selectors. Suppress younger branch training, memory\n  requests and direct divide consumption through the same advance gating.\n  Preserve older memory holds, retained WB forwarding and imem-independent\n  recovery priority. No wrong-path instruction may retire or start divide.\n\n  Drive redirect = late_redirect OR (advance AND direct_miss), where\n  direct_miss is the unchanged predecoded-token predicate. Give the older\n  late event priority. Its redirect target is indirect_target_q. For a\n  direct recovery use only PC+immediate, PC+4, direct-target alignment and\n  the resolved direct condition: choose PC+immediate iff that target is\n  aligned AND (direct JAL OR branch_taken), otherwise PC+4. This direct\n  target expression must exclude jalr_sum, shared misalign_fault and the\n  general actual_next_pc mux. Keep actual_next_pc unchanged for RVFI and\n  architectural EX/MEM state. Remove current-cycle indirect_miss from the\n  fetch recovery event, rather than issuing both immediate and late events.\n\n  Preserve the current speculative next-EX/MEM writer hint, including its\n  direct-JAL alignment qualification. A JALR may now let a younger operand\n  owner be captured, but late_redirect must squash that entire younger EX\n  instruction before any side effect. Keep the existing registered\n  forwarding selectors, load/multiply hazards, ordinary branch training,\n  bimodal predictor and direct offset-four exception. Preserve arithmetic,\n  decoder legality, all pipeline boundaries other than JALR recovery,\n  the direct divide retirement schedule, nret=1 and all external ports.\n\n  The intended gain is splitting the forwarded JALR sum/alignment/recovery\n  path at a real register boundary while avoiding extra cycles and later\n  BHT updates for conditional branches. Dedicated target/event flops avoid\n  making the shared EX/MEM next-PC mux or a live opcode qualification the\n  new recovery source. Costs are target storage, one extra cycle per JALR\n  and recovery control. Divider recurrence or direct-branch paths may cap\n  Fmax; confidence is low until final vendor timing establishes a gain.\n\n  Check aligned, PC+4 and misaligned JALRs, x0/link destinations, back-to-\n  back indirect jumps, both operand forwarding sources, and older dmem\n  holds. Put a store, branch, multiply and DIV/REM immediately after JALR\n  on the wrong path and verify no side effects or predictor training.\n  Exercise JALR targets beginning with DIV/REM and load-to-divide chains\n  against the unchanged ten-cycle retirement-liveness bound. Check reset,\n  imem stalls, exact RVFI PC/operand/result/trap/order and exactly-once\n  recovery. Adapt stage-specific timing tests while retaining every\n  architectural assertion. Run real/ALTOPS pipeline tests, unchanged formal,\n  full-RVFI ready/stalled cosim and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker\n  validation. Measure actual marked cycles and all three Gowin options.\n  Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s.\n\n  Prior and exploratory evidence does not establish a measured win for\n  this final structure. In this slot, a registered fetch buffer reduced\n  cycles but reached only 73.276 MHz median; a parallel-prefix radix-16\n  divider reached 76.8 MHz; replicated pre-forwarding branch comparisons\n  reached 68.316 MHz. Retiming every recovery on the current champion\n  passed all 53 formal checks but reached 75.086 MHz and 4,306,908 cycles.\n  Those changes are excluded. The final hypothesis narrows retiming to\n  JALR, retains immediate direct recovery/training, and gives indirect\n  targets dedicated storage rather than reusing shared EX/MEM pc_next.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add dedicated registered JALR target and pending-event state, preserve\n      immediate direct recovery, and partition redirect target generation\n      so forwarded JALR arithmetic ends at the new target register. Give\n      older pending recovery priority over younger EX advancement/divider\n      launch and insert an inert EX/MEM bubble while retiring the JALR.\n      Preserve trap-aware architectural state and existing writer hints.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add selective-recovery timing, direct-target equivalence, JALR link/\n      misalignment, stalled/reset recovery and wrong-path side-effect tests.\n      Check younger speculative forwarding is squashed before use, exact\n      RVFI fields/order and the unchanged ten-cycle liveness limit for\n      recovered and consecutive divides. Retain all architectural checks.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r12s1.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text())); assert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-002-r12s1';print('Final hypothesis schema validation passed')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
