nd width bounds checked\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r13s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261003-002-r13s1\ntitle: \\\"Eight-cycle divider with width-specialized radix scheduling\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz. Its divider\n  processes four bits per clock through two dependent full-width radix-4\n  stages. CoreMark's measured interval contains no divides, so shortening\n  this physical feedback can improve fitness without additional benchmark\n  cycles. Structural r11s1's fifteen-wide-trial radix-16 design reached\n  only 80.433 MHz and exposed its serial first-nibble prefix. The proposed\n  operand-width partition avoids both fifteen wide trials and that prefix\n  chain while preserving the existing eight iteration clocks and direct\n  retirement schedule. This is one restructuring of the shared divider.\nhypothesis: |\n  Partition the divider by the captured unsigned divisor magnitude, using\n  a registered large_divisor flag for D >= 256. Keep shared 32-bit quotient\n  and remainder state and the existing launch, eight busy edges, retained\n  done and direct-consumption interface. Every RV32M operand is handled;\n  this classification is independent of the benchmark and instruction PC.\n\n  For D >= 256, the top eight quotient bits are provably zero: the top\n  eight dividend bits are at most 255 and therefore less than D. At launch\n  initialize remainder_q to zero-extended dividend_magnitude[31:24] and\n  quotient_q to {dividend_magnitude[23:0], 8'b0}. Process the remaining\n  twenty-four dividend bits with exactly eight radix-8 digit steps, three\n  bits per busy edge. Each ordinary step forms the full 35-bit trial\n  {remainder_q, quotient_q[31:29]}, compares it against D through 7D using\n  seven parallel 36-bit extended subtractions, and uses their borrow bits\n  to select one digit and remainder. Define ge[k] = trial >= kD, w[0] =\n  \"'!ge[1], w[k] = ge[k] && !ge[k+1] for k=1..6, and w[7] = ge[7]. Use masked\n  OR reductions for remainder and digit encoding, with no serial priority\n  selection or second subtraction. Shift quotient by three and append the\n  selected digit. Keep full trial/difference widths through selection;\n  only the selected remainder may narrow to 32 bits because it is below D.\n\n  Prepare wide multiples during the first busy edge, overlapping the first\n  radix-8 digit instead of lengthening launch or adding a setup cycle.\n  The first trial is only dividend_magnitude[31:21], at most 2047. If\n  registered D >= 2048, its first digit is zero and the remainder is that\n  eleven-bit prefix. Otherwise D is 256..2047: compute its first digit\n  with seven parallel narrow trials against k*D[10:0], using 14-bit\n  multiples/trials and 15-bit extended differences. Apply the high-divisor\n  zero-digit choice once after this narrow network, not inside a serial\n  restoring chain. Implement arithmetic and one-hot selection in fabric,\n  not an indexed quotient ROM. On the same edge register full-width 3D,\n  5D and 7D from the registered divisor, using D+2D, D+4D and 8D-D in\n  parallel. Use 35-bit zero extensions before shifts/additions. D, 2D,\n  4D and 6D then come from the divisor and registered 3D by wiring/shifts.\n  The remaining seven busy edges use the single wide radix-8 stage.\n\n  For D < 256, keep all thirty-two dividend bits but exploit remainder\n  width: for every nonzero D, remainder < D <= 255. Initialize quotient_q\n  to the full magnitude and remainder_q to zero. On each of the same eight\n  busy edges consume four dividend bits with two dependent NARROW radix-4\n  steps. Each trial is {remainder[7:0], next_two_dividend_bits}, ten bits;\n  D, 2D and 3D also fit ten bits. Use eleven-bit extended differences and\n  borrow-derived one-hot selection. Capture ten-bit 3D at launch, using\n  explicit zero extension of the low eight magnitude bits. This is a small\n  launch operation; do not create a wide launch-time multiples chain.\n  Keep each selected remainder'\"'s low eight bits and zero-extend it into\n  shared remainder state. This path needs no serial four-step prefix and\n  never places two 32-bit trial chains on one clock. D=0 uses the same\n  bounded schedule with the existing architectural result override.\n\n  Capture signedness, original dividend, quotient/remainder sign and\n  exceptional cases exactly as today. Preserve signed truncation toward\n  zero, dividend-signed remainder, divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 results.\n  Iteration seven still sets done; result selection and sign correction\n  use stable registered state. Preserve all non-divide ALU/multiply logic.\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS retain the exact current formulas and the same\n  eight-iteration handshake; real arithmetic must be tested independently.\n\n  Complete this divider-resource restructuring with a completion grant\n  owned by its registered done token: [REDACTED_SECRET] = div_done &&\n  \"'!normal_wb_valid && !stall && !reset. Keep div_completion.valid driven\n  by this grant. Preserve execute_wait, advance, launch eligibility, older\n  WB priority, dmem holds and all metadata retention. Done already implies\n  that the valid matching divide owns held ID/EX; assert this property.\n  Remove only redundant live is_divide/advance qualification from the\n  consume grant, avoiding an EX opcode-to-retirement-mux path after the\n  arithmetic feedback is shortened. This grant has prior r11s1 precedent;\n  it is not evidence of a standalone performance win. Do not change the\n  direct retirement boundary, add a state, or increase the liveness bound.\n\n  The intended structural gain is one full-width subtract/select depth\n  per large-divisor iteration and two short ten-bit steps for small\n  divisors. Seven wide trials replace the current six, rather than the\n  fifteen in r11s1. Costs are a registered mode, narrow arithmetic,\n  divisor-multiple storage and mode selection into shared state. Signed\n  launch, wide multiple preparation, first-prefix selection, final mode\n  muxing, routing or non-divider paths can still limit Fmax. Keep the\n  width partition and fixed schedule explicit; do not silently fall back\n  to a full-width four-bit chain for any nonzero operand class.\n\n  An independent integer model checked 75,874 unsigned dividend/divisor\n  pairs and 20,000 signed pairs, including dense divisor coverage around\n  255/256 and 2047/2048, against exact quotient and remainder. It checked\n  the eight-iteration schedule, one-hot digit selection, prefix bounds and\n  remainder invariants. This validates the arithmetic construction only;\n  no RTL prototype, formal proof or Gowin measurement of this final\n  divider exists yet. Earlier recovery-address retiming probes in this\n  slot passed correctness checks but measured only 73.573..78.012 MHz;\n  those fetch/branch modifications are excluded from the final hypothesis.\n\n  Retain and extend real arithmetic tests for all four DIV/REM operations,\n  D=0, D=1, signed extrema, powers of two and neighboring values, D=255,\n  256, 257, 2047 and 2048, maximum unsigned operands, q*D plus/minus one,\n  and every radix-8 digit boundary. Exercise maximum-width 7D and changing\n  live inputs after launch. Check reset, held completion, back-to-back\n  operations, both mode transitions and unchanged eight-clock completion.\n  Pipeline tests must cover forwarded operands, load-to-divide, consecutive\n  divides, wrong-path cancellation, older stalled memory, result consumers,\n  exact RVFI operands/results/order and the unchanged ten-cycle liveness\n  limit. Run real/ALTOPS suites, unchanged formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI\n  cosim, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks. Standard\n  ALTOPS formal does not prove the real recurrence; do not claim it does.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, every external port, decoder legality, fetch/prediction,\n  forwarding, memory semantics and all hard invariants. Measure actual\n  bracketed cycles and all three Gowin options. Expected CoreMark cycles\n  remain 4,238,604; at that count acceptance requires median Fmax above\n  86.698653 MHz and fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s, more than\n  4.6 percent over 195.55. The expected gain is a hypothesis, not a measured\n  result, and must survive the unchanged evaluation gates.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the real divider recurrence with the registered divisor-width\n      partition: eight three-bit radix-8 steps for D>=256, overlapping wide\n      multiple preparation with an eleven-bit first prefix, and eight pairs\n      of ten-bit radix-4 steps for D<256. Share architectural quotient and\n      remainder state; use explicit full widths and one-hot selection.\n      Preserve launch/consume, eight busy edges, signs, exceptions, reset,\n      retained completion, exact ALTOPS behavior and all other arithmetic.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Grant divide consumption from registered div_done, with unchanged\n      older-WB, dmem-stall and reset qualification. Keep execute_wait,\n      advancement, launch, held metadata, direct retirement and every other\n      EX function unchanged. Assert that done has a valid matching owner.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing assertion and add independent exact DIV/DIVU/\n      REM/REMU vectors covering both width classes, class/prefix boundaries,\n      every digit, full-width multiples, signs and exceptions. Verify the\n      eight-edge schedule, reset, changing inputs, held done/results and\n      back-to-back mode changes in real and ALTOPS configurations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Extend divide ownership and integration coverage across both divisor\n      classes, forwarding, older memory holds, consecutive and load-dependent\n      divides, wrong paths and all result consumers. Retain exact RVFI,\n      single-retirement order, side-effect and ten-cycle liveness assertions.\n'\"''')\nprint('Wrote final structural hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r13s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261003-002-r13s1\ntitle: \\\"Eight-cycle divider with width-specialized radix scheduling\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz. Its divider\n  processes four bits per clock through two dependent full-width radix-4\n  stages. CoreMark's measured interval contains no divides, so shortening\n  this physical feedback can improve fitness without additional benchmark\n  cycles. Structural r11s1's fifteen-wide-trial radix-16 design reached\n  only 80.433 MHz and exposed its serial first-nibble prefix. The proposed\n  operand-width partition avoids both fifteen wide trials and that prefix\n  chain while preserving the existing eight iteration clocks and direct\n  retirement schedule. This is one restructuring of the shared divider.\nhypothesis: |\n  Partition the divider by the captured unsigned divisor magnitude, using\n  a registered large_divisor flag for D >= 256. Keep shared 32-bit quotient\n  and remainder state and the existing launch, eight busy edges, retained\n  done and direct-consumption interface. Every RV32M operand is handled;\n  this classification is independent of the benchmark and instruction PC.\n\n  For D >= 256, the top eight quotient bits are provably zero: the top\n  eight dividend bits are at most 255 and therefore less than D. At launch\n  initialize remainder_q to zero-extended dividend_magnitude[31:24] and\n  quotient_q to {dividend_magnitude[23:0], 8'b0}. Process the remaining\n  twenty-four dividend bits with exactly eight radix-8 digit steps, three\n  bits per busy edge. Each ordinary step forms the full 35-bit trial\n  {remainder_q, quotient_q[31:29]}, compares it against D through 7D using\n  seven parallel 36-bit extended subtractions, and uses their borrow bits\n  to select one digit and remainder. Define ge[k] = trial >= kD, w[0] =\n  \"'!ge[1], w[k] = ge[k] && !ge[k+1] for k=1..6, and w[7] = ge[7]. Use masked\n  OR reductions for remainder and digit encoding, with no serial priority\n  selection or second subtraction. Shift quotient by three and append the\n  selected digit. Keep full trial/difference widths through selection;\n  only the selected remainder may narrow to 32 bits because it is below D.\n\n  Prepare wide multiples during the first busy edge, overlapping the first\n  radix-8 digit instead of lengthening launch or adding a setup cycle.\n  The first trial is only dividend_magnitude[31:21], at most 2047. If\n  registered D >= 2048, its first digit is zero and the remainder is that\n  eleven-bit prefix. Otherwise D is 256..2047: compute its first digit\n  with seven parallel narrow trials against k*D[10:0], using 14-bit\n  multiples/trials and 15-bit extended differences. Apply the high-divisor\n  zero-digit choice once after this narrow network, not inside a serial\n  restoring chain. Implement arithmetic and one-hot selection in fabric,\n  not an indexed quotient ROM. On the same edge register full-width 3D,\n  5D and 7D from the registered divisor, using D+2D, D+4D and 8D-D in\n  parallel. Use 35-bit zero extensions before shifts/additions. D, 2D,\n  4D and 6D then come from the divisor and registered 3D by wiring/shifts.\n  The remaining seven busy edges use the single wide radix-8 stage.\n\n  For D < 256, keep all thirty-two dividend bits but exploit remainder\n  width: for every nonzero D, remainder < D <= 255. Initialize quotient_q\n  to the full magnitude and remainder_q to zero. On each of the same eight\n  busy edges consume four dividend bits with two dependent NARROW radix-4\n  steps. Each trial is {remainder[7:0], next_two_dividend_bits}, ten bits;\n  D, 2D and 3D also fit ten bits. Use eleven-bit extended differences and\n  borrow-derived one-hot selection. Capture ten-bit 3D at launch, using\n  explicit zero extension of the low eight magnitude bits. This is a small\n  launch operation; do not create a wide launch-time multiples chain.\n  Keep each selected remainder'\"'s low eight bits and zero-extend it into\n  shared remainder state. This path needs no serial four-step prefix and\n  never places two 32-bit trial chains on one clock. D=0 uses the same\n  bounded schedule with the existing architectural result override.\n\n  Capture signedness, original dividend, quotient/remainder sign and\n  exceptional cases exactly as today. Preserve signed truncation toward\n  zero, dividend-signed remainder, divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 results.\n  Iteration seven still sets done; result selection and sign correction\n  use stable registered state. Preserve all non-divide ALU/multiply logic.\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS retain the exact current formulas and the same\n  eight-iteration handshake; real arithmetic must be tested independently.\n\n  Complete this divider-resource restructuring with a completion grant\n  owned by its registered done token: [REDACTED_SECRET] = div_done &&\n  \"'!normal_wb_valid && !stall && !reset. Keep div_completion.valid driven\n  by this grant. Preserve execute_wait, advance, launch eligibility, older\n  WB priority, dmem holds and all metadata retention. Done already implies\n  that the valid matching divide owns held ID/EX; assert this property.\n  Remove only redundant live is_divide/advance qualification from the\n  consume grant, avoiding an EX opcode-to-retirement-mux path after the\n  arithmetic feedback is shortened. This grant has prior r11s1 precedent;\n  it is not evidence of a standalone performance win. Do not change the\n  direct retirement boundary, add a state, or increase the liveness bound.\n\n  The intended structural gain is one full-width subtract/select depth\n  per large-divisor iteration and two short ten-bit steps for small\n  divisors. Seven wide trials replace the current six, rather than the\n  fifteen in r11s1. Costs are a registered mode, narrow arithmetic,\n  divisor-multiple storage and mode selection into shared state. Signed\n  launch, wide multiple preparation, first-prefix selection, final mode\n  muxing, routing or non-divider paths can still limit Fmax. Keep the\n  width partition and fixed schedule explicit; do not silently fall back\n  to a full-width four-bit chain for any nonzero operand class.\n\n  An independent integer model checked 75,874 unsigned dividend/divisor\n  pairs and 20,000 signed pairs, including dense divisor coverage around\n  255/256 and 2047/2048, against exact quotient and remainder. It checked\n  the eight-iteration schedule, one-hot digit selection, prefix bounds and\n  remainder invariants. This validates the arithmetic construction only;\n  no RTL prototype, formal proof or Gowin measurement of this final\n  divider exists yet. Earlier recovery-address retiming probes in this\n  slot passed correctness checks but measured only 73.573..78.012 MHz;\n  those fetch/branch modifications are excluded from the final hypothesis.\n\n  Retain and extend real arithmetic tests for all four DIV/REM operations,\n  D=0, D=1, signed extrema, powers of two and neighboring values, D=255,\n  256, 257, 2047 and 2048, maximum unsigned operands, q*D plus/minus one,\n  and every radix-8 digit boundary. Exercise maximum-width 7D and changing\n  live inputs after launch. Check reset, held completion, back-to-back\n  operations, both mode transitions and unchanged eight-clock completion.\n  Pipeline tests must cover forwarded operands, load-to-divide, consecutive\n  divides, wrong-path cancellation, older stalled memory, result consumers,\n  exact RVFI operands/results/order and the unchanged ten-cycle liveness\n  limit. Run real/ALTOPS suites, unchanged formal, ready/stalled full-RVFI\n  cosim, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/marker checks. Standard\n  ALTOPS formal does not prove the real recurrence; do not claim it does.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, every external port, decoder legality, fetch/prediction,\n  forwarding, memory semantics and all hard invariants. Measure actual\n  bracketed cycles and all three Gowin options. Expected CoreMark cycles\n  remain 4,238,604; at that count acceptance requires median Fmax above\n  86.698653 MHz and fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s, more than\n  4.6 percent over 195.55. The expected gain is a hypothesis, not a measured\n  result, and must survive the unchanged evaluation gates.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the real divider recurrence with the registered divisor-width\n      partition: eight three-bit radix-8 steps for D>=256, overlapping wide\n      multiple preparation with an eleven-bit first prefix, and eight pairs\n      of ten-bit radix-4 steps for D<256. Share architectural quotient and\n      remainder state; use explicit full widths and one-hot selection.\n      Preserve launch/consume, eight busy edges, signs, exceptions, reset,\n      retained completion, exact ALTOPS behavior and all other arithmetic.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Grant divide consumption from registered div_done, with unchanged\n      older-WB, dmem-stall and reset qualification. Keep execute_wait,\n      advancement, launch, held metadata, direct retirement and every other\n      EX function unchanged. Assert that done has a valid matching owner.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing assertion and add independent exact DIV/DIVU/\n      REM/REMU vectors covering both width classes, class/prefix boundaries,\n      every digit, full-width multiples, signs and exceptions. Verify the\n      eight-edge schedule, reset, changing inputs, held done/results and\n      back-to-back mode changes in real and ALTOPS configurations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Extend divide ownership and integration coverage across both divisor\n      classes, forwarding, older memory holds, consecutive and load-dependent\n      divides, wrong paths and all result consumers. Retain exact RVFI,\n      single-retirement order, side-effect and ten-cycle liveness assertions.\n'\"''')\nprint('Wrote final structural hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Wrote final structural hypothesis: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r13s1.yaml\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
