{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261002-002-r4s1','hyp-20261003-001-r11s0','hyp-20261003-001-r11s2','hyp-20261003-003-r13s2'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'], d['title'])\n  h=d['hypothesis'];print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261002-002-r4s1 Separate pipeline validity from payload to shorten redirect recovery\nMake one structural change: represent pipeline occupancy independently\nof instruction payload and enforce side effects at valid instruction\nboundaries. Remove the combinational redirect-to-NOP-to-decode path\nand the requirement to clear wide payload registers when squashing an\ninstruction. Keep the present stage count, prediction policy, divider\nschedule, single retirement channel and memory protocol.\n\nDrive the combinational IF/ID instruction directly from imem_data.\nAn unavailable fetch is represented by validity, not a replacement\ninstruction. EX recovery updates PC with highest nonreset priority\nand clears ID/EX validity; it must not change the instruction bits,\nregister-file read addresses, immediate or decoded data in that cycle.\nKeep predictor targets and metadata paired with each accepted PC.\nCorrect predictions still have no recovery bubble, and mispredictions\nstill squash exactly the same younger instruction as today.\n\nSplit ID/EX payload updates from its valid-bit update. Reset or a\npermitted flush clears validity. An older dmem hold or active divide\nretains the current instruction. A load-use bubble clears validity\nwhile preserving payload; ordinary issue captures raw payload and\nfetch validity. No redirect-driven mux or clear may remain on the\noperand, immediate, source-register or instruction payload flops.\nPayload reset may remain for deterministic simulation, independently\nof recovery. Apply the same valid/payload separation to EX/MEM bubbles\nwhile older instructions drain during a divide.\n\nBecause invalid entries can now contain real control bits, qualify\nevery architectural action and hazard with the owning entry's\nvalidity: load-use detection, EX/MEM memory blocking and requests,\nregister writes, forwarding eligibility, divider launch, redirects,\npredictor updates and RVFI retirement. Invalid entries cannot start\na divide, issue a store, forward a destination or create a stall.\nLoad-use source compariso\n…[14709 of 17781 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a95770c173d97fe1]…\n direction changes, +4/zero/negative offsets, source addresses differing in upper tag bits and full-width target wrap. Change the instruction at a cached PC to a new immediate, opposite direct kind, ordinary ALU op, load/store, JALR, reserved branch and illegal opcode; verify unconditional stale recovery and exact architectural RVFI results. Cover correct and stale predictions during imem stalls, older dmem holds, load/multiply-use hazards and divide recovery. Check all architectural assertions, contiguous retirement order, exactly-once memory effects and training, and no wrong-path stores/divide starts. Retain unchanged formal including its ten-cycle liveness bound, real and ALTOPS pipeline/cosim gates, and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/markers. Measure actual marked cycles and all three Gowin options; acceptance requires median Fmax_Hz times 10/cycles strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s. Inspect timing to establish that live imem target arithmetic is absent from ordinary next-fetch selection.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
