{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"agent_message","text":"The refinement is also below the champion in the first completed placement. A dynamic CoreMark trace shows 135,320 adjacent load-to-branch dependencies in the measured interval. I’m shifting the proposal to later branch operand consumption, which could remove some of those bubbles while separating comparison from EX forwarding."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r15s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261003-002-r15s1\ntitle: \\\"Late branch operand collection at the memory completion boundary\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion achieves 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz with 4,238,604\n  marked CoreMark cycles. Its EX forwarding, branch comparison and\n  recovery form one combinational timing cone. A branch immediately\n  following a load also pays the generic late-result dependency bubble.\n  A dynamic retirement trace of the canonical marked interval contains\n  2,922,016 instructions, 529,588 conditional branches and 135,320\n  adjacent load-to-branch dependencies on a nonzero destination. This\n  is an opportunity count, not a count of removable cycles: instruction\n  stalls already separate some pairs. Moving branch operand consumption\n  to the following stage can remove these dependencies' explicit bubbles\n  and place a register boundary before comparison and recovery.\n  Prior r10s1 merely registered already-resolved recovery events; it\n  added 1.61 percent CoreMark cycles without removing load-use bubbles.\n  The proposed late operand collection is the essential difference.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make one structural change: resolve conditional branches at the\n  EX/MEM-to-MEM/WB completion stage, with a registered late-operand\n  selector that can consume the immediately preceding load or multiply\n  from normal MEM/WB. Move architectural recovery for direct and\n  indirect jumps to this same in-order completion boundary so no live\n  EX recovery remains on the fetch and squash control path. Ordinary\n  ALU, memory, multiply and divide execution keep their current cycle\n  boundaries. Keep nret=1, the existing BHT prediction algorithm,\n  architectural register file and external memory/RVFI interfaces.\n\n  In decode, classify only validated conditional BRANCH encodings as\n  late consumers: opcode 0x63 with funct3 in 0,1,4,5,6,7. Exempt only\n  those instructions from the current load/multiply-use bubble. Loads,\n  stores, ALU operations, JALR, reserved encodings and all other consumers\n  retain their present dependency interlock. Preserve imem-unavailable\n  masking, dmem hold priority and divide waiting. No unavailable or\n  wrong-path instruction may gain architectural ownership through this\n  exemption. Do not use raw opcode matching that accepts reserved\n  BRANCH funct3 values.\n\n  EX continues collecting the ordinary forwarded source values. For a\n  conditional branch, register those values and source addresses into\n  EX/MEM without consuming them in a comparator or deciding a branch\n  trap there. Also capture two late-source bits on the same advancing\n  edge. Each bit is true iff the current valid EX/MEM instruction will\n  actually write a nonzero rd at normal MEM/WB on that edge and its rd\n  matches the branch's corresponding source. Qualify with MEM's actual\n  trap-suppressed next_mem_wb_w_en and valid ownership; a misaligned\n  load must not become a replacement source. Computing these matches\n  before the branch's EX/MEM boundary avoids a destination comparison\n  in its eventual compare/recovery cycle. This can apply to all normal\n  preceding writers; loads and multiplies are the cases where replacing\n  the collected EX operand is necessary for correctness.\n\n  In MEM, a branch's resolved operand is the stable normal MEM/WB\n  completed value when its registered late-source bit is set, otherwise\n  its EX/MEM-collected source value. Use one two-input mux per source.\n  Both bits may select the same preceding result. The normal MEM/WB\n  value here is the existing retained register, not the current MEM\n  combinational result or the top-level direct-divide retirement mux.\n  There must be no combinational memory-data-to-branch-comparator bypass.\n  Thus a load enters MEM while its dependent branch is in EX; at the\n  next edge the load result and the branch's late-source selectors\n  register together; the branch compares that registered result in MEM\n  with no load-use bubble. A multiply follows the same protocol.\n\n  When an older memory request stalls, hold the branch in ID/EX with\n  its existing forwarding state and hold EX/MEM normally. Capture the\n  late-source bits only when that producer is accepted and the branch\n  actually enters EX/MEM. Preserve retained WB forwarding while the\n  branch is still in EX. A trapped preceding load must leave the\n  destination's prior architectural value visible: collect its fallback\n  using the unchanged ordinary forwarding priority, and leave its late\n  replacement bit clear. Divides continue to deliver to younger decode\n  through the existing RF write-first path; they do not occupy the\n  normal late-source slot.\n\n  Carry the branch operation and the existing recovery tokens through\n  EX/MEM: branch_prediction_xor, recovery_branch_check and\n  recovery_static_miss. Precompute/register PC+4 and PC+immediate in EX,\n  independent of operand comparison. MEM performs exactly the current\n  signed/unsigned/equality condition, direction-token mismatch and\n  taken-target alignment checks using its resolved operands. Preserve\n  offset-plus-four equivalence, predicted-but-untaken recovery and\n  fall-through on a misaligned taken target. A not-taken branch with a\n  misaligned hypothetical target still neither traps nor loses its\n  legitimate training event. Update both RVFI source values and pc_next\n  from MEM's resolved branch values; stale EX placeholders must never\n  reach architectural retirement. Train the same bimodal table exactly\n  once for each advancing legal, nontrapping conditional branch.\n\n  For JAL/JALR, retain EX's present forwarded target arithmetic,\n  alignment handling and PC+4 link result. Register the existing direct\n  miss or unconditional legal-JALR recovery token and resolved target\n  with the instruction, then issue its recovery in MEM. Preserve the\n  current trap-aware speculative writer qualification, including direct\n  JAL alignment suppression; do not replace it with unconditional writer\n  intent. Unconditional legal-JALR recovery now kills a younger EX\n  instruction before that instruction can consume a speculative link\n  from a trapping JALR. Keeping every recovery at MEM avoids a new\n  priority network combining older MEM recovery with younger live EX\n  recovery. Do not change target prediction, add a BTB/RAS, or add a\n  separate delayed BHT update stage.\n\n  A recovery from the older MEM instruction must redirect fetch and\n  squash both the younger ID/EX instruction and the next EX/MEM entry.\n  The recovering instruction still advances to MEM/WB and retires once.\n  Clear side-effect controls as well as validity in every killed entry.\n  Gate younger EX advancement, divider launch and divider consumption\n  with the older recovery. Gate execute_wait with its negation so a\n  wrong-path divide cannot block recovery; an unlaunched wrong-path\n  divide must never start. Recovery overrides younger dependency holds\n  and imem unavailability, while actual older dmem transactions retain\n  their hold priority. Emit redirect and training only from valid,\n  accepted MEM ownership, with reset cancellation and no replay of a\n  retained event. Correctly predicted branches sustain one instruction\n  per cycle, including consecutive conditional branches.\n\n  This structure separates forwarding/operand collection from the\n  branch compare-to-fetch path while retaining only a small late-result\n  mux before comparison. Its throughput benefit specifically comes\n  from removing branch late-result bubbles. Costs include branch target\n  and selector storage, one additional cycle per recovery, and BHT\n  training one cycle later. These costs must be measured together:\n  135,320 adjacent load/branch pairs are not a promised 135,320-cycle\n  saving. The unchanged divider, jump target capture or new MEM compare\n  path may limit Fmax. Confidence is low; expected net fitness gain is\n  eight percent, not a measured result.\n\n  Check branches after loads of every width/sign and after all MUL*\n  variants, either or both source matches, x0, repeated writes to the\n  same destination and both ordinary forwarding sources. Include a\n  misaligned load whose destination retains an earlier value. Check\n  long dmem holds, imem bubbles, branch chains, taken and untaken\n  misaligned targets, offset +4, JAL/JALR link consumers and traps,\n  reset, and direct divide completion followed by a branch. Put loads,\n  stores, branches, multiplication and DIV/REM immediately behind a\n  mispredicted branch or recovering jump and require no wrong-path\n  memory effects, divider launches, training or retirements. In\n  particular check an older branch recovery overriding a younger divide\n  wait and a recovered target starting with DIV/REM against the unchanged\n  ten-cycle retirement-liveness property.\n\n  Adapt internal stage-location and operand-timing test oracles to MEM\n  resolution while retaining independent architectural RVFI checks,\n  exact memory effects, arithmetic assertions and all decoder legality\n  checks. Run real/ALTOPS unit tests, unchanged single-channel formal,\n  full-RVFI ready/stalled cosim and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/address/\n  marker validation. Arithmetic equations and divide latency are not\n  changed. Inspect the mapped circuit to confirm the branch comparator\n  reads EX/MEM and normal MEM/WB registers, and that no live EX redirect\n  or dmem-data bypass recreates the old path.\n\n  This exact late-consumer architecture has not been implemented or\n  timed. Exploratory retirement-bypass retiming in this slot passed all\n  53 standard formal checks, ready/stalled selftest and three random\n  programs, and canonical CoreMark at the unchanged 4,238,604 cycles,\n  but reached only 77.630 MHz median. It is excluded from this proposal.\n  A related squash-separated operand probe also supplies no evidence\n  of acceptance. Restore all exploratory RTL before handing off this\n  hypothesis. Measure actual marked cycles and the median of all three\n  Gowin placement options for the final architecture. Acceptance needs\n  fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s; resource savings or\n  fewer dependency bubbles alone do not establish a win.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Extend EX/MEM with two registered late-source selectors, branch\n      prediction/recovery tokens and jump recovery ownership. Carry\n      registered branch target and fall-through data through the real\n      completion boundary without altering architectural port types.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route valid, trap-qualified next-WB ownership to EX's branch\n      source-match capture. Connect MEM recovery and training to fetch\n      and hazards and send the older recovery to EX for squash priority.\n      Classify validated incoming conditional branches for the selective\n      late-result hazard exemption; preserve nret=1 and retirement muxes.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Exempt only validated conditional branches from load/multiply-use\n      interlocks because they consume operands one stage later. Preserve\n      all other hazards, dmem/divide holds, imem semantics and recovery\n      priority, using MEM's accepted recovery event.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Collect branch source values, register actual next-WB source\n      matches, targets and prediction tokens, and defer comparison and\n      branch alignment traps to MEM. Register JAL/JALR recovery events.\n      On older MEM recovery suppress younger execution/divide launch\n      and inject an inert EX/MEM bubble while permitting older retirement.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Resolve conditional operands with the registered normal-WB late\n      bypass, perform branch comparison, traps and recovery here, and\n      retire corrected source values and next PC. Emit accepted jump\n      recovery and exactly-once branch training from the same boundary.\n      Retain load/multiply completion, memory handshakes and WB holding.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check late load/multiply-to-branch operands, trap-cancelled writers,\n      branch/jump recovery precedence and wrong-path cancellation with\n      adversarial bus holds. Update internal stage-timing expectations\n      while preserving exact RVFI, arithmetic, memory effects, legality\n      and the unchanged ten-cycle liveness assertions.\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-002-r15s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Revised structural hypothesis written and schema validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
