ng direct\n  miss or unconditional legal-JALR recovery token and resolved target\n  with the instruction, then issue its recovery in MEM. Preserve the\n  current trap-aware speculative writer qualification, including direct\n  JAL alignment suppression; do not replace it with unconditional writer\n  intent. Unconditional legal-JALR recovery now kills a younger EX\n  instruction before that instruction can consume a speculative link\n  from a trapping JALR. Keeping every recovery at MEM avoids a new\n  priority network combining older MEM recovery with younger live EX\n  recovery. Do not change target prediction, add a BTB/RAS, or add a\n  separate delayed BHT update stage.\n\n  A recovery from the older MEM instruction must redirect fetch and\n  squash both the younger ID/EX instruction and the next EX/MEM entry.\n  The recovering instruction still advances to MEM/WB and retires once.\n  Clear side-effect controls as well as validity in every killed entry.\n  Gate younger EX advancement, divider launch and divider consumption\n  with the older recovery. Gate execute_wait with its negation so a\n  wrong-path divide cannot block recovery; an unlaunched wrong-path\n  divide must never start. Recovery overrides younger dependency holds\n  and imem unavailability, while actual older dmem transactions retain\n  their hold priority. Emit redirect and training only from valid,\n  accepted MEM ownership, with reset cancellation and no replay of a\n  retained event. Correctly predicted branches sustain one instruction\n  per cycle, including consecutive conditional branches.\n\n  This structure separates forwarding/operand collection from the\n  branch compare-to-fetch path while retaining only a small late-result\n  mux before comparison. Its throughput benefit specifically comes\n  from removing branch late-result bubbles. Costs include branch target\n  and selector storage, one additional cycle per recovery, and BHT\n  training one cycle later. These costs must be measured