e counter MSB to\\nchoose between PC+4 and PC+sign_extended_B_immediate. Always predict legal\\ndirect JAL to PC+sign_extended_J_immediate. Other instructions, including\\nJALR, retain sequential prediction. Fetch already exposes the instruction\\ncombinationally to decode, so this needs no new pipeline stage, target cache,\\n\n…[277787 of 280859 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:451758d6ddffdfb3]…\nJALR target arithmetic still drives the fetch-PC data input. The deliberate\\nrecovery bubble for a sequential or trapping JALR can cost cycles on instruction\\nstreams other than this CoreMark workload. Placement results are sensitive to\\nequivalent combinational factoring; the numbers above are measurements of the\\nfinal RTL, not the supplied prototype's results.\\n\\nFinal RTL fingerprint (`sha256sum cores/bench/rtl/*.sv | sha256sum`):\\n`f12aac79ecd272c2c4d79f76861efe153b88470eea91f3dfda2b9d5c19ae9f43`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T23:28:27.455958Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r10s2 (improvement, +9.93%): Predecoding direct recovery into ID/EX tokens and unconditionally recovering legal JALRs lets squash and speculative next_ex_mem_w_en bypass forwarded target arithmetic/alignment, improving Fmax with unchanged CoreMark cycles; the owner hint remains safe only when advancing JALRs clear younger ID/EX controls and forwarding selectors while held JALRs disable capture.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
