{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor r in map(json.loads,Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()):\n if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID:',r['id'],'TITLE:',r['title'],'OUTCOME:',r.get('outcome'),'FITNESS:',r.get('fitness'))\n  print('MOTIVATION:',r.get('motivation',''))\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes','')[:13000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 TITLE: Early direct-control-flow prediction with a small bimodal table OUTCOME: improvement FITNESS: 13.38\nMOTIVATION: The baseline predicts every control transfer sequentially and flushes on every\ntaken conditional branch, JAL, or JALR. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so a\ntaken transfer normally wastes one issue opportunity, not two. CoreMark's\nrepeated loops and direct calls should make many of these bubbles avoidable.\nThe only recorded experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is absent and there\nare no previous predictor results. The measured critical path is through\nforwarding and the combinational ALU, so this proposal targets iterations per\ncycle while keeping added prediction logic out of that arithmetic path.\n\nNOTES: \n\nID: hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 TITLE: Prediction-driven fetch ahead across execute backpressure OUTCOME: regression FITNESS: 13.99\nMOTIVATION: The previous predictor hypothesis improved fitness to 13.38 by adding a\n128-entry bimodal table and early direct-JAL targets. Its measured critical\npath still runs through the execute ALU. A fresh trace of the unchanged\nchampion reproduces 4,202,272 bracketed CoreMark cycles and contains\n2,922,016 instructions, 529,588 conditional branches and only 13,735 JALRs.\nAn architectural branch-trace replay estimates 54,467 direction misses\nfor the existing bimodal table; a larger table saves only about 15,000.\nThese are replay estimates, not exact pipeline misprediction counters.\nBetter direction or return prediction alone has insufficient cycle-saving\nheadroom for the required 4.6 percent gain. The valuable unused capability\nof the existing predictor is producing a speculative fetch stream while\ndecode is blocked: today stall_if freezes the fetch PC on load-use and\ndata-memory stalls even when the instruction bus could deliver data.\n\nNOTES: \n\nID: hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 TITLE: Compact prediction-token fetch ahead on the iterative-divider core OUTCOME: regression FITNESS: 78.61\nMOTIVATION: The champion already predicts direct JAL and co\n…[11029 of 14101 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7470cc4e919092e6]…\n**, exactly\nthe champion's cycle count. Final fitness:\n\n`82.887e6 * 10 / 4,238,604 = 195.55259231577188 iterations/second`.\n\nThis exceeds **186.07294 iterations/second**, and improves on 177.89 by\n**9.92894%**. At this cycle count the required median is 78.868951 MHz.\n\nOption-zero resources: **5,048 logic cells, 1,832 registers, 0 LUT-RAM, 4 BSRAM,\n6 DSP**. Its worst reported setup path starts at the benchmark instruction LFSR\nand ends at ID/EX `recovery_static_miss`, through IF and ID.\n\n## Remaining considerations\n\nJALR target arithmetic still drives the fetch-PC data input. The deliberate\nrecovery bubble for a sequential or trapping JALR can cost cycles on instruction\nstreams other than this CoreMark workload. Placement results are sensitive to\nequivalent combinational factoring; the numbers above are measurements of the\nfinal RTL, not the supplied prototype's results.\n\nFinal RTL fingerprint (`sha256sum cores/bench/rtl/*.sv | sha256sum`):\n`f12aac79ecd272c2c4d79f76861efe153b88470eea91f3dfda2b9d5c19ae9f43`.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
