{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID',r['id'],r.get('outcome'),r.get('fitness'),r['title'])\n  print('MOTIVATION:',r['motivation'])\n  print('NOTES:',r.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 improvement 13.38 Early direct-control-flow prediction with a small bimodal table\nMOTIVATION: The baseline predicts every control transfer sequentially and flushes on every\ntaken conditional branch, JAL, or JALR. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so a\ntaken transfer normally wastes one issue opportunity, not two. CoreMark's\nrepeated loops and direct calls should make many of these bubbles avoidable.\nThe only recorded experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is absent and there\nare no previous predictor results. The measured critical path is through\nforwarding and the combinational ALU, so this proposal targets iterations per\ncycle while keeping added prediction logic out of that arithmetic path.\n\nNOTES: \n\nID hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 regression 13.99 Prediction-driven fetch ahead across execute backpressure\nMOTIVATION: The previous predictor hypothesis improved fitness to 13.38 by adding a\n128-entry bimodal table and early direct-JAL targets. Its measured critical\npath still runs through the execute ALU. A fresh trace of the unchanged\nchampion reproduces 4,202,272 bracketed CoreMark cycles and contains\n2,922,016 instructions, 529,588 conditional branches and only 13,735 JALRs.\nAn architectural branch-trace replay estimates 54,467 direction misses\nfor the existing bimodal table; a larger table saves only about 15,000.\nThese are replay estimates, not exact pipeline misprediction counters.\nBetter direction or return prediction alone has insufficient cycle-saving\nheadroom for the required 4.6 percent gain. The valuable unused capability\nof the existing predictor is producing a speculative fetch stream while\ndecode is blocked: today stall_if freezes the fetch PC on load-use and\ndata-memory stalls even when the instruction bus could deliver data.\n\nNOTES: \n\nID hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 regression 78.61 Compact prediction-token fetch ahead on the iterative-divider core\nMOTIVATION: The champion already predicts direct JAL and conditional branches with a\n128-entry bimodal table. Prior branch-trace analysis\n…[11724 of 14796 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8466348b3fde8818]…\n`f12aac79ecd272c2c4d79f76861efe153b88470eea91f3dfda2b9d5c19ae9f43`.\n\n\nID hyp-20261003-003-r11s2 regression 183.4 Split direct-prediction target arithmetic from its fast range certificate\nMOTIVATION: The champion reaches 195.55 iterations/second at 82.887 MHz with\n4,238,604 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its latest reported worst\npath starts at the instruction source and passes through IF into\nID/EX recovery_static_miss. The predictor currently forms a full\n32-bit PC-plus-sign-extended-immediate sum before reducing its upper\ntwelve bits to decide whether a taken prediction is safe. That late\ndecision feeds both fetch selection and the newly registered recovery\ntokens. Prior branch replay found only about 54,467 conditional misses\nand 13,735 JALRs; accuracy alone offers insufficient headroom. Fetch\nqueues repeatedly lost their saved cycles to timing, and pipelining\nBHT training regressed. Shorten prediction eligibility itself while\nretaining the champion's successful recovery and prediction policy.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
