{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_56", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'] in ('hyp-20261002-003-r8s2','hyp-20261002-003-r5s2'):\n  h=r['hypothesis']\n  print(r['id'],h['hypothesis'] if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r5s2 Add exactly four entries of predicted instruction fetch-ahead buffering\nto the current frontend. Preserve the 128-entry bimodal direction table,\nearly direct JAL prediction, legal predecode and target guards. The one\narchitectural change is buffering the predicted stream while decode is\nblocked. Do not change the divider, pipeline depth, direction policy,\nindirect-jump policy, architectural memory semantics or retirement width.\n\nEach entry stores only the full 32-bit instruction, full 32-bit PC and\nfrozen predicted_direct bit. Use a fixed head in entry zero and compact\nentries toward the head on a dequeue, rather than reading a circular\narray through a pointer-indexed payload mux. Decode selects entry zero\nwhen occupied, otherwise the raw bus response. Empty-queue fall-through\nmust preserve present zero-wait issue latency. The saved direction bit\nreaches EX unchanged and uses the champion's direction-XOR recovery with\nits offset +4 exception. Existing predicted_next_pc may be reconstructed\nfrom a queued entry only as verification metadata; it must not feed the\ntimed recovery or autonomous fetch-PC path. Never redo a BHT lookup for\na queued instruction. Keep full-width PC arithmetic and target semantics.\n\nSeparate fetch bus readiness from instruction availability at decode.\nAvailability is queue_nonempty OR io_imemReady. Give IF the decode hold\nfor load-use, dmem backpressure and execute_wait, independently of raw\ninstruction-bus readiness. With a saved head, decode can issue even when\nio_imemReady is zero. Compute source addresses and head selection without\ndependence on dequeue/hold so load-use detection has no combinational\nfeedback. Preserve load-use bubbling, older dmem hold priority and the\nretained MEM/WB forwarding fields.\n\nCandidate consumption is availability AND NOT decode_hold. Pop only\nwhen consuming a buffered head. Accept a raw bus response only when\nio_imemReady and either occupancy is below four or a simultaneous pop\ncreates space. Bypass a response directly when the queue is\n…[7539 of 10611 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:58780b666dba485c]…\n liveness, PC consistency, uniqueness and\nregister consistency. All eight full-RVFI comparisons of selftest and\nrandom1/2/3 passed with ready buses and combined instruction/data stalls.\nA 6,000-cycle independent IF queue/BHT model test passed, including\n466 full pop/push events, 257 buffered deliveries during raw bus stalls,\n572 recoveries and 3,241 boundary cycles. Strict Verilator lint passed.\nRepeating all three Gowin options after a constant-width lint cleanup\nreproduced the same frequencies. Option zero used 4,895 logic cells,\n1,928 registers, zero LUT-RAM, four BSRAM and six DSP blocks.\n\nThese are exploratory measurements, not approval of the eventual RTL.\nRequire unchanged formal and RVFI cosim, directed queue tests, canonical\nstalled CoreMark and all three vendor placement options on the final\nimplementation. Fitness must exceed 157.97738 iterations/second,\n4.6 percent above 151.03; at 3,991,298 cycles this requires median Fmax\nabove 63.05348 MHz. Preserve nret=1, every port and all hard invariants.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
