{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"The target-cache prototype also regressed, to 74.064 MHz. I’m changing the final proposal to a registered predicted-stream buffer. Earlier queues retained an empty-buffer bypass, leaving the long fetch-to-decode path intact; the proposed buffer would remove that bypass and use buffering to offset the extra recovery cycle. I’ll mark it as unmeasured and low confidence."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-003-r12s2',\n'title':'Register the predicted instruction stream before decode',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 195.55 iterations/second at 82.887 MHz with 4,238,604 marker-bracketed cycles. Earlier branch replay found only about 54,467 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs, so more accurate direction or return prediction has insufficient headroom. Earlier predicted-stream queues hid roughly five percent of stalled CoreMark cycles, but retained an empty-queue combinational bypass from instruction memory to decode. Their timing cost prevented adoption on later champions. The current core still combines instruction predecode, prediction, instruction masking, decoder and register-file access before ID/EX. A genuine register boundary for the predicted stream can remove that bypass path while allowing useful fetches during execution backpressure.\n\nFresh isolated exploration rejected conservative target-range certificates, separate B/J target arithmetic, smaller bimodal tables and an instruction-validated direct-target cache. Their best tested median was 80.968 MHz, below 82.887 MHz; none established an improvement. These circuits are not included. The final hypothesis instead changes the predictor delivery boundary, a distinction from all prior fall-through queues in the recorded history.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Introduce one two-entry, fixed-head, fully registered buffer between the existing predicted fetch stream and decode. Every instruction accepted from imem must cross this boundary before reaching the decoder; there is no empty-queue combinational instruction bypass. This is one predictor-delivery change: retain the existing 128-entry bimodal table, immediate-based direct JAL prediction, aligned low-1-MiB target guard, sequential JALR policy, EX recovery tokens and single retirement channel. Preserve arithmetic, register-file implementation, forwarding algorithm and memory completion.\n\nEach entry holds full PC[31:0], instruction[31:0] and the predicted_direct bit that actually selected its next fetch address. Retain predicted_next_pc as saved verification metadata, or reconstruct it exclusively from the saved instruction, PC and direction for that purpose. Never consult the current BHT for a buffered entry. Entry zero is always the oldest instruction; entry one is the tail. Decode's instruction/source-address inputs come only from the registered head or a constant NOP when empty. Do not add any raw-imem bypass even on a simultaneous empty push/pop. Both an empty first fetch and a fetch after recovery become visible to decode on the following cycle.\n\nSeparate raw io_imemReady from decode availability. Expose available=(count\"'!=0), independent of redirect and decode hold. Feed available to hazard_unit'\"'s existing instruction-availability input, while preserving load/multiply-use, dmem and divide hold priority. Supply IF with the raw bus-ready signal and decode_hold=stall_id. Normal consume is available AND NOT decode_hold. The current head and its raw source addresses must remain independent of consume to avoid a hazard combinational loop. When empty, decode captures an inert bubble using the existing unavailable-instruction behavior; when held, the head remains stable.\n\nNormal acceptance is raw_ready AND (count<2 OR consume), subject to the exceptional-address serialization below. On consume, shift a retained tail into the fixed head. Append an accepted fetch at old count minus consume, with append winning over a shift to the same slot. A simultaneous full pop/push preserves count two. A push to an empty buffer cannot be consumed on the same edge. Advance the full-width fetch PC using the raw response's pre-edge prediction whenever that response is accepted, even during load-use, dmem or divide holds. Correctly predicted branches and JALs preserve the buffered stream; only misses cause recovery. Do not gate ordinary fetch acceptance with global stall_if, which now includes buffer emptiness.\n\nReset and EX redirect take priority over queue occupancy, pending boundary state and fetch-PC updates. Redirect atomically empties both slots, installs the full architectural recovery address, and retains the existing synchronous ID/EX control and forwarding-selector squash. Payload bits may be written on a redirect edge if occupancy makes them unreachable, allowing redirect to clear validity rather than reset every payload flop. Empty-buffer output must nevertheless mask stale instruction controls with a constant NOP. Preserve EX's existing advancement gate: older dmem backpressure defers redirect and training, and a recovery that can advance overrides raw imem unavailability. Retain exactly-once branch training, retirement, accepted stores and divide launch/consume semantics.\n\nUse full-width saved PCs to avoid architectural-address truncation. Ordinary speculative run-ahead is allowed only for aligned fetch PCs below 0x000ffffc. At the last in-range word, an unaligned PC or an out-of-range PC, serialize delivery: accept a response only into an empty buffer, save its prediction, and hold the live fetch PC with a pending-advance flag until that saved instruction is consumed by decode. While pending, forbid another raw acceptance. Consumption then advances to the saved predicted_next_pc, unless recovery overrides it. This is the ordinary demand advance delayed to decode consumption, not autonomous look-ahead across a boundary. The next fetch still crosses the register boundary. Never clamp, wrap into a smaller address space or replace architectural trap/target behavior. Recovery must clear the pending flag and always remain able to make progress.\n\nThe expected benefit combines hiding ready imem opportunities during execution stalls with removing raw fetch/prediction paths from decode and register-file capture. Costs are one extra initial delivery cycle and one extra lost issue opportunity per misprediction/JALR recovery. The prior branch counts suggest roughly 1.6 percent extra recovery cycles against about five percent potential stall savings, but those are estimates from earlier designs, not a measured cycle count for this registered buffer. Whether the new boundary improves Gowin placement enough is uncertain. Expected fitness gain is seven percent with low confidence. No prototype of this final registered-buffer design has been simulated or timed; the earlier local predictor experiments are negative evidence, not validation of this proposal.\n\nValidate an independent cycle-level FIFO/PC/prediction model, then real and ALTOPS pipeline suites, unchanged single-channel formal including the ten-cycle liveness property, full-RVFI ready/stalled cosim, and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/marker/bounds checks. Specifically verify divider retirement spacing with the added delivery boundary; do not change any formal bound. Measure the actual bracketed cycles and all three Gowin placement options. Require median Fmax_Hz times 10/cycles strictly above 204.5453 iterations/second, more than 4.6 percent over 195.55. Keep only the registered two-entry design in this hypothesis; do not combine it with target arithmetic, BHT sizing, arithmetic-unit or retirement changes.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add the two-entry fixed-head registered predicted-stream buffer with full PCs, frozen prediction metadata, explicit raw-ready/decode-hold inputs and an availability output. Eliminate raw instruction bypass to decode. Implement full simultaneous push/pop, reset/redirect occupancy priority and serialized exceptional-address delivery with a pending saved-PC advance. Preserve existing direct prediction, BHT state/training and full-width recovery targets.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire raw io_imemReady to IF acceptance and buffer availability to hazard instruction readiness. Feed stall_id back as decode hold instead of using the aggregate stall_if to block raw fetch. Keep the decoder and register-file source addresses driven solely by the registered IF head. Preserve all top-level ports, nret=1, forwarding ownership and retirement wiring.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Interpret instruction readiness as registered-head availability rather than raw bus readiness. Keep all existing load/multiply-use, dmem, divide and recovery priorities; unavailable head emits a bubble, while execution holds preserve the head and ID/EX. Document that stall_if is no longer the fetch engine's acceptance gate. Avoid adding any combinational path from raw imem data to hazard source-address checks.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Add an independent registered FIFO/PC/prediction reference model covering empty push latency, one/full simultaneous pop/push, blocked decode with continued fetch, delivery during raw imem stalls, and frozen prediction tokens across BHT updates. Check reset, deferred and immediate recovery, wrong-path loads/stores/divides, both forwarding sources, strict RVFI PC/order and exactly-once effects. Exercise the final instruction-memory word, high and unaligned full-width PCs and redirect while boundary advance is pending. Adapt cycle observations to the added boundary without weakening architectural assertions, and verify unchanged ten-cycle divider liveness with ready buses.'''},\n]}\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r12s2.yaml')\ns=yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=92,allow_unicode=True)\np.write_text(s)\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Wrote and validated final registered-buffer hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
