{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d.get('outcome'),d.get('fitness'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('MOTIVATION:',d.get('motivation',''))\n  print('LESSON:',d.get('lesson',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Early direct-control-flow prediction with a small bimodal table improvement 13.38 5.623 4202272\nMOTIVATION: The baseline predicts every control transfer sequentially and flushes on every\ntaken conditional branch, JAL, or JALR. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so a\ntaken transfer normally wastes one issue opportunity, not two. CoreMark's\nrepeated loops and direct calls should make many of these bubbles avoidable.\nThe only recorded experiment is the baseline; LESSONS.md is absent and there\nare no previous predictor results. The measured critical path is through\nforwarding and the combinational ALU, so this proposal targets iterations per\ncycle while keeping added prediction logic out of that arithmetic path.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (improvement, +10.40%): With combinational IF/ID, a 128-entry bimodal BHT and early JAL targets improve CoreMark by eliminating the single issue bubble on correctly predicted transfers, using EX comparison of actual_next_pc against the latched predicted_next_pc to flush only on misprediction.\nhyp-20261002-003-r2s2 Prediction-driven fetch ahead across execute backpressure regression 13.99 5.567 3980323\nMOTIVATION: The previous predictor hypothesis improved fitness to 13.38 by adding a\n128-entry bimodal table and early direct-JAL targets. Its measured critical\npath still runs through the execute ALU. A fresh trace of the unchanged\nchampion reproduces 4,202,272 bracketed CoreMark cycles and contains\n2,922,016 instructions, 529,588 conditional branches and only 13,735 JALRs.\nAn architectural branch-trace replay estimates 54,467 direction misses\nfor the existing bimodal table; a larger table saves only about 15,000.\nThese are replay estimates, not exact pipeline misprediction counters.\nBetter direction or return prediction alone has insufficient cycle-saving\nheadroom for the required 4.6 percent gain. The valuable unused capability\nof the existing predictor is producing a speculative fetch stream while\ndecode is blocked: today stall_if freezes the f\n…[10214 of 13286 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1310bbe769d435c4]…\ninstruction predecode, prediction, instruction masking, decoder and register-file access before ID/EX. A genuine register boundary for the predicted stream can remove that bypass path while allowing useful fetches during execution backpressure.\n\nFresh isolated exploration rejected conservative target-range certificates, separate B/J target arithmetic, smaller bimodal tables and an instruction-validated direct-target cache. Their best tested median was 80.968 MHz, below 82.887 MHz; none established an improvement. These circuits are not included. The final hypothesis instead changes the predictor delivery boundary, a distinction from all prior fall-through queues in the recorded history.\nLESSON: - 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.90%): A fully registered two-entry fetch queue removes the raw-imem decode path but leaves EX-to-fetch-PC recovery and count-to-hazard-to-head feedback near critical, so its 3.51% CoreMark cycle savings are outweighed by median Fmax falling from 82.887 to 77.652 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The history leaves little headroom for better prediction accuracy, and fetch queues have repeatedly lost their cycle savings to lower Fmax. I’m focusing on recovery timing: when a direct prediction is wrong, its saved direction identifies the correction address, so the forwarded branch result need only trigger recovery."}}
