{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"The tested recovery rewrites all lost Fmax, so I’m excluding them from the final proposal. The remaining promising angle is a PC-indexed target cache with validation in decode: it would remove live instruction-target arithmetic from fetch selection while preserving recovery from stale entries."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r13s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-003-r13s2',\n 'title':'PC-only direct-target prediction with decode-time stale-entry recovery',\n 'category':'predictor',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz and 4,238,604 marker-bracketed cycles. Its recorded option-zero critical path starts at the instruction source and crosses IF into ID/EX recovery_static_miss. Current direct prediction decodes the live instruction, adds its immediate to the PC and checks the full target range before selecting the next fetch. Earlier branch replay found only about 54,467 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs, so greater direction accuracy has little headroom. Queues, target-range rewrites and delayed BHT training have already regressed.\n\nLocal recovery experiments in this slot also lost timing: prediction-selected combinational correction reached 73.302 MHz median; registering its address reached 79.413 MHz; separate equality/ordered recovery enables reached 75.842 MHz; moving static recovery classification into IF reached 64.564 MHz. Those experiments are excluded. This hypothesis instead removes live instruction data from the ordinary prediction-address selection by using a PC-only target lookup. Unlike the instruction-validated target cache explored in r12s2, cache validation happens at ID/EX and never gates the fetch-address choice.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace immediate-based taken-target selection in IF with one 32-entry, direct-mapped branch target cache, indexed by PC[6:2]. Keep the existing 128-entry bimodal direction table and its existing exactly-once training policy. Each target entry contains a valid bit, full remaining source-PC tag PC[31:7], target word address [19:2], direct-kind bit (conditional branch or JAL), and the signed direct immediate signature imm[20:1]. All installed source PCs and targets must be word aligned. An entry is installed only for legal BRANCH or direct JAL with a non-+4 immediate and a full-width computed target in [0, 0x00100000). Consequently every installed target is nonsequential for its tagged PC, including full-width wraparound cases, and the compressed target reconstructs exactly as {12'b0, target_word, 2'b00}. Do not truncate the source tag or the architectural PC.\n\nLookup uses only the registered fetch PC and stored predictor state. On a tag hit, predict the cached target for cached JAL, or for cached BRANCH when the existing BHT counter MSB is taken. Otherwise predict PC+4. A miss has no live-immediate target fallback: the branch/JAL resolves through the existing EX recovery boundary and trains the cache. In particular, do not validate the live instruction, recompute its target, or test its target range in the fetch selection path. Cold and conflicting direct-JAL misses add a recovery bubble; this is an explicit performance cost. Keep IF/ID combinational and add no fetch queue or pipeline stage.\n\nUse modest fabric storage with constant-index per-entry updates and a balanced read structure. A suitable implementation gates each entry's target, signature and kind by valid AND matching index AND matching full tag, then OR-reduces the leaves. Direct mapping guarantees at most one match. Reset valid bits; uninitialized payload must be masked so it cannot affect prediction or leak X into an accepted bundle. Keep the direction-table implementation unchanged. Cache training is one synchronous write/invalidation on an advancing EX instruction, independent of branch outcome for target allocation. Allocate a legal direct branch/JAL whenever its source/target alignment, target range and non-+4 checks pass, whether a branch is taken or not. Otherwise invalidate only a matching source-PC entry. Never train from bubbles, squashed instructions, held EX instructions, or JALR; a legal JALR may invalidate an old matching direct entry. Read-before-write is acceptable because acceptance freezes the pre-edge lookup result.\n\nCarry the exact predicted_next_pc, a used_nonsequential_cache_prediction bit, and the selected immediate signature with the accepted instruction. In decode, the authoritative decoder determines whether this actual instruction is a legal BRANCH/direct JAL. A cached prediction matches its actual direct target iff that legal-direct classification holds and the cached signature equals the decoded imm[20:1]. The source-PC tag was checked at fetch, so equal signed immediates guarantee equal full-width targets without a second target addition or a wide next-PC comparison. Branch/JAL kind need not match the cached kind: identical immediate and PC identify the same target. Branch funct3 legality must still be checked by the existing decoder.\n\nDefine stale_prediction = used_nonsequential_cache_prediction AND NOT target_signature_match. For a fresh matching cache prediction, feed predicted_direct=1 into the champion's existing recovery-token equations. For sequential prediction, feed predicted_direct=0. For a stale prediction, force recovery_static_miss=1 and disable recovery_branch_check; the instruction must recover unconditionally when it advances, regardless of its actual opcode or branch outcome. This also corrects stale predictions on ordinary arithmetic, loads, stores, illegal instructions, reserved branch encodings and changed direct immediates. Do not assume instruction memory is immutable. Always retain the architectural actual_next_pc computation as the correction address, including PC+4 for non-control instructions and misalignment traps. RVFI next PC and link values continue to use actual architectural behavior. Preserve unconditional recovery for every advancing legal JALR, including PC+4 JALRs, so its speculative forwarding-owner safety argument remains valid.\n\nPrediction consumption must coincide with ID/EX acceptance. Preserve reset, load/multiply-use, divide and dmem hold priorities; held instructions retain the complete prediction certificate. Advancing recovery overrides imem unavailability and squashes all younger controls and forwarding selectors. Wrong-path instructions may read the predictor but cannot allocate/invalidate entries, launch a divider, issue a data transaction or retire. A stale prediction changes control flow speculatively only; the actual instruction itself still executes and retires exactly once, in order.\n\nGuard speculative sequential advancement at the final in-range word. If an accepted fetch at 0x000ffffc has no nonsequential cache prediction, hold the fetch PC and set a boundary-wait flag instead of exposing speculative 0x00100000. Emit an inert younger bubble while waiting. Carry a force-recovery bit with that accepted instruction, and when it can advance in EX, recover to its unmodified full-width actual_next_pc even if the usual prediction comparison would report a match. Reset or recovery clears boundary-wait; an older recovery cancels this younger boundary instruction normally. A cache-predicted in-range transfer from the boundary can proceed normally. This only serializes the otherwise unsafe boundary case: it must not clamp, wrap or alter an architectural next PC, and recovery/ready handling must still guarantee progress. Test this case explicitly with cold JAL, taken conditional branch, stale entry and bus holds.\n\nThis is one predictor-architecture change: lookup before instruction inspection, followed by decode-time validation and the existing EX correction boundary. Preserve arithmetic units, register file, forwarding algorithm, memory completion, nret=1, decoder legality and external IO. The intended Fmax gain comes from replacing live-imem decode/add/range/select feedback with stored-tag/target selection. Costs are roughly 2,080 cache payload/valid bits before synthesis, additional lookup/validation logic and target-cache misses. Expected fitness gain is eight percent with low confidence; this final cache architecture has not been implemented, simulated or timed. The negative local experiments are not validation of it.\n\nVerify an independent target-cache/prediction model including reset, tag aliases, row replacement, simultaneous lookup/training and held fetches. Exercise repeated calls and loops, direction changes, +4/zero/negative offsets, source addresses differing in upper tag bits and full-width target wrap. Change the instruction at a cached PC to a new immediate, opposite direct kind, ordinary ALU op, load/store, JALR, reserved branch and illegal opcode; verify unconditional stale recovery and exact architectural RVFI results. Cover correct and stale predictions during imem stalls, older dmem holds, load/multiply-use hazards and divide recovery. Check all architectural assertions, contiguous retirement order, exactly-once memory effects and training, and no wrong-path stores/divide starts. Retain unchanged formal including its ten-cycle liveness bound, real and ALTOPS pipeline/cosim gates, and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/markers. Measure actual marked cycles and all three Gowin options; acceptance requires median Fmax_Hz times 10/cycles strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s. Inspect timing to establish that live imem target arithmetic is absent from ordinary next-fetch selection.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Replace live-immediate target prediction with the specified 32-entry fully tagged direct-target cache and existing bimodal direction lookup. Freeze selected signature, exact predicted PC and cache-use metadata on acceptance. Add training/invalidation inputs and boundary-wait serialization; keep reset/recovery/hold priorities and bounded cached targets.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Extend IF/ID and ID/EX prediction metadata with cache-use/signature and boundary force-recovery information as needed. Keep full architectural PCs, all retirement bundles and nret=1 unchanged.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Validate cached immediate signatures against the authoritative decoded legal direct instruction. Derive matching/sequential recovery tokens, force unconditional stale-prediction recovery even for non-control or illegal instructions, and capture boundary recovery. Hold or clear the whole certificate with ID/EX.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Extend recovery causes with stale prediction and serialized-boundary recovery while retaining the architectural actual_next_pc correction target and unconditional legal JALR recovery. Export exactly-once direct-target allocation and matching-entry invalidation metadata from advancing instructions; preserve BHT training, traps, forwarding ownership and division.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire target-cache update/invalidation and boundary/prediction metadata between IF, ID and EX. Preserve top-level ports, existing hazard interfaces and retirement arbitration; boundary wait must deliver a NOP/invalid younger payload without blocking the accepted instruction from advancing.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add an independent cache/PC model, stale-instruction and full-tag alias tests, cold/warm control transfers, simultaneous update/lookup and bus/execute holds. Validate boundary serialization, wrong-path suppression, exact RVFI PC/order/trap/link fields and existing liveness. Adapt prediction-specific observations to the new certificate while retaining every architectural assertion.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=92))\nprint(p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
