{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d.get('outcome'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  s=h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else str(h)\n  notes=d.get('implementation_notes','')\n  for t in (s,notes):\n   for p in t.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n    if any(k in p.lower() for k in ['replay','accuracy','return','gshare','ras','percent','probe']): print(p[:5000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Early direct-control-flow prediction with a small bimodal table improvement 5.623 4202272\nAdd one instruction-aware direct-control-flow predictor: a 128-entry bimodal\nbranch history table with two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[8:2],\ncombined with early B/J immediate target calculation. Initialize counters to\nweakly not taken. For a legal conditional BRANCH, use the counter MSB to\nchoose between PC+4 and PC+sign_extended_B_immediate. Always predict legal\ndirect JAL to PC+sign_extended_J_immediate. Other instructions, including\nJALR, retain sequential prediction. Fetch already exposes the instruction\ncombinationally to decode, so this needs no new pipeline stage, target cache,\ntarget tags, return stack, or global history. Compute predictions from raw\nimem_data and the PC, never from a flush-generated NOP. Validate BRANCH\nfunct3 before predicting; the authoritative decoder and trap rules remain\nunchanged. Only speculate to word-aligned targets in instruction memory\n[0x00000000, 0x00100000); otherwise predict PC+4 and let EX resolve normally.\nCarry the exact predicted_next_pc with the instruction through IF/ID and\nID/EX. Use it for both the accepted fetch's PC update and that instruction's\nmetadata. Recompute the actual next PC in EX from forwarded operands and\nexisting trap rules, including PC+4 on misalignment. Redirect and flush only\nwhen a valid, advancing EX instruction's actual next PC differs from its\nrecorded prediction. Thus a correct taken prediction accepts the target\ninstruction without a bubble, a falsely taken prediction returns to PC+4,\nand an unpredicted taken transfer redirects to its resolved target. RVFI\npc_wdata must continue to contain the actual next PC, never the prediction.\nExpect roughly 9 percent fewer bracketed CoreMark cycles, giving about\n10 percent higher fitness if median Fmax stays near baseline. This is an\nestimate, not a measurement: direction aliasing, imem stalls overlapping\nredirect bubbles, and FPGA placement changes can reduce the benefit.\nKeep the\n…[19769 of 22841 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b9fb7131f23e595a]…\nnsecutive branches to the same index, opposite outcomes, saturation and same-cycle lookup/update consequently reproduce the reference exactly; no training event is dropped or applied twice. This is stronger than accepting a one-event-stale table, and it requires the read forwarding even though the measured CoreMark trace happened to be insensitive to one clock of stale training in the simpler probe.\nThe intended timing gain comes from terminating late EX training at a small event register, moving bank/row decoding before that boundary, and localizing counter/read selection. The coherent read path preserves the original prediction sequence and should preserve all 4,238,604 measured cycles, so acceptance needs median Gowin Fmax above 86.698653 MHz, yielding fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s. Expected gain is seven percent with low confidence: registered-update experiments have exposed other limiting paths, and the extra counter-bit read plus forwarding mux may offset the bank organization's benefit.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
