{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"The baseline recheck reproduced 82.887 MHz. I’m narrowing the final proposal to a prediction-aware prefix resolver: fold the saved prediction into small parallel comparisons and predecode the recovery condition. This aims to remove logic after the wide comparison; the expected gain will remain explicitly unmeasured and low confidence."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r15s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-003-r15s2',\n'title':'Prediction-polarity prefix resolution with predecoded recovery rails',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 195.55 iterations/s at 82.887 MHz with 4,238,604 marked CoreMark cycles. A fresh unchanged-RTL Gowin placement-option-1 build reproduced 82.887 MHz. Its worst path runs from MEM/WB operand data through EX forwarding, a full-width carry chain, recovery logic and high-fanout squash to the registered forwarding selectors. Prior replay found only about 54,467 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs, so accuracy alone cannot clear the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. Target caches, delayed BHT training and extra recovery boundaries have regressed. Target the computation of prediction mismatch itself, preserving the champion's prediction sequence and recovery cycle.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the conditional-branch predictor resolver with one prediction-polarity parallel-prefix network. This is a predictor recovery architecture change, not a direction-policy change. Keep the original 128-entry binary bimodal BHT, immediate-resolution training, raw-instruction direct-target prediction, full-width guarded targets, combinational IF/ID, pipeline depth and unconditional legal JALR recovery. Do not carry over any exploratory BHT recoding, static prediction, delayed recovery, arithmetic-readiness qualification or forwarding-comparator replication.\n\nThe current recovery first computes a full-width equality/less-than branch_base, selects the comparison kind, XORs the saved branch_prediction_xor, and qualifies the result with recovery_branch_check. Instead, predecode equality recovery polarity into ID/EX and absorb ordered-comparison prediction polarity into the leaves of a balanced prefix tree. Keep all existing prediction metadata, architectural branch semantics and full-width actual_next_pc calculations.\n\nLet P be the saved branch_prediction_xor = predicted_direct XOR branch_op[0]. Retain the existing signed-to-unsigned bias on bit 31 of each forwarded operand: XOR that sign bit with \"'!branch_op[1], leaving the other bits unchanged. Divide these biased operands into sixteen two-bit slices. For each slice i independently compute E_i=(A_i==B_i) and M_i=((A_i<B_i) XOR P). Each leaf is a two-bit comparison with saved polarity, rather than a slice of a 32-bit ripple comparator. A pair (E,M) summarizes equality and the less-than-result XOR P for its entire bit range.\n\nCombine adjacent summaries in exactly four balanced levels, always giving the more significant half priority: E=E_hi AND E_lo; M=E_hi ? M_lo : M_hi. Implement this as explicit small constant-index nodes, with a two-input mux or the equivalent disjoint masked OR. The root M is exactly (biased_rs1 < biased_rs2) XOR P, including the all-equal case where every leaf and the root M equal P. The root E is exact 32-bit equality. This associative lexicographic reduction propagates the already prediction-adjusted predicate, so the ordered recovery path has no full-width carry chain and no polarity XOR after the root. Use ordinary synthesizable logic, without changing constraints or requiring preservation attributes.\n\nIn decode, derive three mutually exclusive recovery enables from the existing legal/aligned/non-+4 recovery_branch_check qualification. For BEQ/BNE, register recover_equal when P=0 and recover_unequal when P=1. For BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU, register recover_ordered. All three must be zero for any nonbranch, reserved encoding, misaligned direct target or +4 branch. Capture these bits with the same reset, flush and hold priorities as the original ID/EX recovery metadata. Continue capturing P exactly as today; never consult a later BHT lookup for a held instruction.\n\nIn EX, compute direct_miss = recovery_static_miss OR (recover_equal AND E_root) OR (recover_unequal AND !E_root) OR (recover_ordered AND M_root). Preserve the original advance gate and the separate unconditional legal-JALR miss. Do not move decode, target alignment, the branch-kind mux or a late direction XOR back between the ordered prefix root and recovery. Keep direct JAL, +4 direct transfers, target-range refusal, misalignment traps, recovery priority and wrong-path squash behavior unchanged.\n\nArchitectural branch outcome and training must remain exact. Reconstruct unsigned-order less-than as L_root=M_root XOR P for use in the existing branch_base/branch_cond selection, or use an algebraically identical independent outcome expression. BEQ/BNE use E_root; ordered branches use L_root; retain funct3 inversion and reserved-encoding handling. Keep that outcome reconstruction outside the direct_miss cone. RVFI pc_wdata and redirect_target continue to use the architectural resolved next PC, never a predicted value. BHT training still occurs exactly once on each advancing legal nontrapping conditional branch, including untaken branches with a misaligned hypothetical target, and never on holds or bubbles.\n\nThe intended gain is shorter forwarded-operand-to-recovery latency at unchanged CPI, with sixteen small leaves and four regular reduction levels replacing the ripple comparison followed by selection and polarity logic. New decode tokens cost at most three ID/EX bits and can replace timed uses of the old generic branch-check token. Main risks are routing through the prefix tree, polarity fanout, logic duplication for architectural outcome, and the possibility that BHT training or the unchanged divider becomes limiting. Expected fitness gain is seven percent with low confidence. This final prediction-polarity tree and predecoded-rail combination has NOT been implemented, simulated or timed; no measured improvement is claimed.\n\nExploratory negative evidence must not be mistaken for validation of this proposal. Direction/strength BHT recoding measured 73.126 MHz median; an ordinary four-by-eight-bit comparator without absorbed prediction polarity or predecoded recovery rails measured 74.169 MHz median; selective registered conditional recovery measured 72.814 MHz median and increased marked cycles to 4,293,094. Their canonical CoreMark checks passed, but all failed the fitness target. Forwarding-parallel comparisons, static prediction and removing the generic execute-wait gate from recovery likewise failed the frequency target in placement-option-1 screening. The final proposal changes both the representation of the predicate inside the comparison tree and its registered recovery interface, rather than repeating those circuits.\n\nBefore implementation acceptance, prove the prefix algebra against independent signed/unsigned RV32 branch comparisons for arbitrary operands, both saved prediction polarities and all six legal funct3 values. Verify the complete new direct_miss expression against the original token predicate, including offset +4, zero/backward offsets, source-PC wrap, out-of-range prediction refusal and alignment traps. Exercise equal operands, every possible first-differing slice, opposite sign bits, x0, all forwarding selections, held WB forwarding, load/multiply-use stalls, older dmem stalls, unavailable imem, wrong-path stores/divides and reset. Preserve the existing real/ALTOPS unit tests and unchanged single-channel formal, including ten-cycle liveness, full-RVFI cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Prediction, redirect and retirement sequences should remain identical to the champion for a fixed ready trace.\n\nKeep nret=1, external IO, decoder defaults, arithmetic units, register-file implementation and every correctness gate intact. Measure all three Gowin placement options and the actual marked cycles on the final RTL. At unchanged 4,238,604 cycles, acceptance requires median Fmax strictly above 86.698653 MHz and fitness strictly above 204.5453 iterations/s. A cycle-only or single-placement gain is insufficient.'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add recover_equal, recover_unequal and recover_ordered bits to id_ex_t. Retain saved prediction polarity and existing architectural/verification fields; no top-level interface or retirement-width change.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Predecode the three mutually exclusive recovery enables from the existing validated branch, alignment, non-+4 and prediction-polarity conditions. Register, hold and clear them with ID/EX under the original priorities.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Implement sixteen two-bit prediction-adjusted comparison leaves and a four-level lexicographic prefix tree. Form direct_miss from the registered recovery enables and root equality/mismatch, while reconstructing exact architectural branch outcome outside that recovery cone. Preserve all advancement, training, JALR, target, trap, forwarding and divider behavior.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Extend independent predicate-equivalence and recovery tests across both prediction polarities, signed/unsigned boundaries, every first-differing slice and forwarding combination. Update packed ID/EX layout observations for the new bits without weakening architectural, stall, training, RVFI or wrong-path assertions. Check identical accepted prediction and redirect traces against the old resolver.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=95))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
